Executive Summary

Background

     The World Bank tackles the decarbonization of international maritime transport from two
     intertwined angles. On a global level, it supports the International Maritime Organization
     (IMO) policymaking process through targeted analytics and advisory. On a country level,
     the World Bank assists its member countries in identifying business and development
     opportunities. Here, Morocco has been identified as one of the countries with the highest
     potential to become a future supplier of green hydrogen-based shipping fuels to the global
     fleet of vessels.

     As part of this engagement with the Government of Morocco, the World Bank supported
     the development of prefeasibility studies for four ports: Tanger Med on the Mediterranean
     coast, and Mohammedia, Jorf Lasfar and a potential port near Tan-Tan on the Atlantic coast.
     The objective was to understand the opportunities, challenges, and requirements of developing
     green hydrogen-based shipping fuel value chains in Morocco. By making the findings available
     to public and private stakeholders, the studies are to serve as one of many building blocks to
     seize this unique growth opportunity for the country. In 2024, two in-person and two virtual
     stakeholder workshops with more than 100 Moroccan and international experts significantly
     benefited this work.

Key findings
As countries are committed to reduce their greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions, green hydrogen1 is projected to be in high demand and Morocco is well-placed to become a global supplier. Green hydrogen will be especially critical in so-called hard-to-abate sectors such as heavy-duty transport, chemicals (including fertilizers), and heavy industries.

Morocco has abundant renewable energy resources and is strategically located on key maritime trade routes. This has sparked much private sector interest in Morocco as a producer and exporter of green hydrogen or derivatives such as green ammonia and methanol. These fuels are touted as the leading fuels for decarbonizing shipping at large scale (World Bank, 2021b). Various international reports deem the solar and wind potential in Morocco to be among the best in the world, enabling the country to produce green hydrogen at a lower cost than many other countries (IRENA, 2022a;
IRENA, 2022b; IEA, 2023b; Deloitte, 2023).

    Different methods of hydrogen production are distinguished by naming the hydrogen a different color. Generally, hydrogen
    produced using fossil fuels is called gray, black, or brown hydrogen, while hydrogen produced from renewable energy sources is
    usually called green hydrogen.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade              xiii

The green hydrogen economy and maritime transport depend on each other. Maritime transport which will need to run on green hydrogen-based fuels to a large extent will also be crucial in facilitating the development of the global green hydrogen trade. Where pipelines are either technically unfeasible or financially unviable, ships and ports will be the only options to link future low-cost production centers such as North Africa, Southern Africa, or Latin America with future high-demand consumption centers like Europe or East Asia. They will be needed for the import and export of large key components of any green hydrogen value chain, such as wind blades or electrolyzers, too. With Morocco implementing the National Hydrogen Roadmap (Ministère de la
Transition Energétique et du Développement Durable, 2021) and releasing the recent Morocco Offer,
“L’Offre Maroc”, it is essential to examine the key role its ports can play in developing these new value chains.

This report assesses how selected ports can contribute to Morocco’s emerging green hydrogen economy and become part of a future value chain. These ports are Tanger Med, Mohammedia,
Jorf Lasfar, and a port in the vicinity of Tan-Tan. Other ports in Morocco have been or are being analyzed similarly in separate, complementary studies.

This report analyzes:

•   Lowest production cost of green hydrogen in the vicinity of the selected ports.

•   Possible competition from other regional producers.

•   Potential domestic and regional market demand from shipping, local industry, and
    export markets.

•   Each port’s competitive advantages and disadvantages.

•   The technical and financial viability of potential investment projects.

The results serve as an input into a comprehensive scenario analysis aimed to identify a Lighthouse
Scenario that connects and embeds the ports in an integrated value chain. This is to reduce the overall cost of green hydrogen-based fuel production, storage, supply, and export, while also servicing other industries.

Finally, the report recommends measures at the national and individual port levels to advance green hydrogen-based shipping fuel projects and discusses how to enhance the competitiveness of these fuels made in Morocco.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                                               xiv

The findings of the report can be summarized in three key messages:

Key message 1: Ports serving international shipping, local industry, and export markets can be critical facilitators of
Morocco’s emerging green hydrogen economy.
This report focuses on three primary demand centers for green hydrogen and derivatives at ports: international shipping, local industry, and exports (primarily to the European Union (EU))
(see Figure E.1). The demand levels are estimated by considering current demand, regulations on a national, regional, and international scale, and possible competition from other producers in the region. They are then adjusted and scaled to project future demand for green hydrogen-based fuels made in Morocco.

International shipping: EU policies and policy measures under the 2023 IMO GHG Strategy to fully decarbonize shipping by or around 2050 are expected to drive significant demand for green hydrogen-based shipping fuels. In 2025, the IMO approved a technical standard on the GHG content of shipping fuels and an economic instrument pricing GHG emissions from ships. These measures still need to be adopted.

By 2030, ships visiting Morocco’s ports could require around 0.2 million tons (mt) of hydrogen equivalent, and approximately 2.83 mt by 2050. In terms of energy, this is equivalent to
28 Petajoules (PJ) and 401 PJ respectively. This projection is based on an analysis of current shipping traffic at Morocco’s ports, passing coastal traffic, and future projections for fleet growth and green shipping fuel adoption. Demand at the port of Tanger Med is expected to account by far for the largest share.2 This is not surprising, as Tanger Med is currently the only commercial bunkering port in Morocco, with annual volumes of about 1.6 mt of fossil-based shipping fuel (67 PJ). In the medium and long term, Moroccan ports have the potential to capture additional bunkering demand from the transit traffic through the Strait of Gibraltar and along the Atlantic coast.

Local industry: Ports are likely to serve as clean energy supply hubs for local industry and facilitate their decarbonization efforts. Jorf Lasfar and Mohammedia are located close to green hydrogen-
prone industries such as fertilizers, iron and steel, and aviation.

OCP, one of the world’s leading fertilizer producers based in Morocco, plans to produce up to 3.2 mt of ammonia (72 PJ) for its own use by 2032 (La Tribune Afrique, 2023). Up to 0.15 mt of green hydrogen (21 PJ) may be supplied to Morocco’s iron and steel industry, if direct reduced iron was to be produced in the future. The aviation industry could create additional demand for green hydrogen which is needed to produce sustainable aviation fuels like synthetic kerosene, though this report does not quantify this demand yet.

Export markets: Ports are also expected to facilitate the connection between low-cost production centers and high-demand consumption centers of green hydrogen derivatives. Morocco is well positioned to export large quantities of these derivatives to key European import hubs such as
Antwerp, Hamburg, or Rotterdam.

    Fuel demand is expressed in terms of the hydrogen content of each fuel instead of tons of hydrogen derivative to facilitate the
    comparison across scenarios and fuels. Though methanol is expected to be in slightly higher demand at Tanger Med initially,
    demand for ammonia is expected to grow significantly. For example, the Medium Demand scenario projections show 9 mt of
    methanol demand and almost 7 mt of ammonia demand at Tanger Med by 2050.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               xv

The REPowerEU plan and the EU Hydrogen Strategy have set ambitious targets for the EU to become an important producer as well as large-scale importer of green hydrogen. The EU anticipates up to €500 billion worth of investments to flow into the green hydrogen sector by 2050.

Despite competition from other Mediterranean countries, this report estimates that European demand will be so large that Morocco could export up to 6.9-9.5 mt of hydrogen (1,346 PJ)
equivalent by 2050.

All this results in a triple role for ports in the context of Morocco’s emerging green hydrogen economy.

