Key message 3: The Government of Morocco can maximize the contribution of Moroccan ports to its national green hydrogen economy through developing regulation and common-user infrastructure.
Morocco can help narrow today’s financial viability gap between green hydrogen-based and fossil fuels. In maritime transport, there is a cost-competitiveness gap between currently dominant fossil fuels, primarily very low sulfur fuel oil, and clean alternatives such as green hydrogen-based fuels.
For the equivalent energy content, green fuels are about three times as expensive as fossil fuels.
To close or at least narrow this gap, several levers need to be pulled both on the demand side and the supply side. Appropriate measures to minimize environmental and social risks associated with green hydrogen projects should be taken, too.

On the demand side, stringent policy measures—mostly international—can foster the uptake of green hydrogen-based fuels. The IMO has approved a marine fuel standard requiring ships to lower their fuel’s GHG intensity over time and an economic instrument that prices GHG emissions above the GHG fuel intensity target. The IMO is developing detailed implementation guidelines for the policy measures and over time, it will set new compliance targets and review the measures. This process provides opportunities for the Government of Morocco to proactively shape the policy development.
Other policy options include contract-for-difference schemes, where subsidies initially cover the difference between higher production cost and the market’s current willingness to pay for green hydrogen-based fuels. Private sector initiatives, such as voluntary customer-paid premiums for green transportation services, can also boost demand.

On the supply side, green fuel production costs can be reduced. It is advantageous to develop common-user infrastructure for the production, storage, distribution, and use of green hydrogen at the lowest costs and with the highest synergies possible. This can be achieved, amongst others, by:

•   Developing port infrastructure to import large renewable energy equipment such as wind blades
    and export green hydrogen-based fuels.

•   Building desalination plants to provide large amounts of freshwater to electrolyzers and serve
    local communities.

•   Planning a pipeline backbone to move hydrogen cost-effectively and at large scale across
    the country.

•   Mobilizing concessional financing and securing long-term offtake agreements to reduce project
    risks and attract private investment.

Additional measures could include generating emissions reduction credits under Article 6 of the
Paris Agreement, minimizing administrative burdens to facilitate investments, or providing fiscal incentives. For a subset of green fuels like green methanol for ships or sustainable aviation fuels for planes, it is also important that public or private stakeholders identify an affordable and sustainable
source of carbon as critical feedstock to contain overall fuel production cost.
