First, to facilitate the emergence and deployment of green hydrogen-based shipping fuels, the
Government of Morocco should ensure that both international and national regulations not only address environmental and social risks but also foster an attractive investment climate.
International regulations have a significant impact on the uptake of green shipping fuels—now and over the coming decades. Moroccan policymakers can co-shape the evolving regulatory landscape, for instance, through proactive participation in the policymaking process at the IMO. Additionally, national guidelines for the safe handling of green hydrogen-based fuels need to be developed to provide clarity and ensure safety in ports and their surroundings.

Second, the viable production of green hydrogen-based fuels requires addressing the affordable and sustainable sourcing of key feedstocks. This includes securing desalinated water for green hydrogen production, managing the disposal of brine in an environmentally sound manner, and sourcing sustainable CO2 for green methanol or sustainable aviation fuels. The sourcing of CO2 remains a challenge globally. It is also essential to assess and train the workforce to ensure they possess the necessary skills for handling these fuels. By investigating value chains, scaling production, and optimizing operational processes based on lessons learned from smaller projects, the safe and efficient production of these fuels can be ensured.

Third, the Government of Morocco can also play a key role in developing common-user infrastructure.
The availability of common-user infrastructure helps attract private investments and maximize the development co-benefits of hydrogen investments. This includes port infrastructure built to import large renewable energy equipment such as wind blades and to export green hydrogen and its derivatives. Other examples are desalination plants to provide fresh water to electrolyzers and local communities in water-scarce areas, hydrogen storage in salt caverns accessible to multiple users, or a pipeline backbone to transport hydrogen cost-effectively between key users across the country.

