Pilot projects
In addition to these recommendations, several promising pilot projects have been identified.
The pilot project in Tanger Med is outlined and explained in full in the main report.

To gain experience with operational procedures and regulations, the proposed Tanger Med pilot project would explore the feasibility of ship-to-ship bunkering using green hydrogen-based fuels.
It appears beneficial to begin with such a bunkering pilot at Tanger Med since many ships calling at this port also call at ports in the European Union before or after. Based on current EU regulation, some of Tanger Med’s shipping activities are already subject to the rules of the EU ETS.3 To continue attracting these vessels, Tanger Med may therefore be well advised to begin offering green hydrogen-based fuels enabling ships to reduce their overall journey’s GHG emissions. Otherwise,
Tanger Med may gradually risk losing customers to other ports offering such fuels. The physical execution of the pilot is anticipated to take one year in total, commencing in 2027 once feasibility planning and designs are specified. The capital and operational expenditures of running the bunkering pilot for a year are estimated at approximately €6.6 million.

