Introduction

International shipping plans to decarbonize by or around 2050. Morocco can position itself as a producer and exporter of zero-carbon fuels, specifically green hydrogen-based fuels, especially given its geographical advantages.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                 2

Morocco has the potential to become a major producer of green hydrogen. For many sectors, including the shipping industry, green hydrogen is a clean alternative to traditional fossil-based fuels and is expected to be in high demand as countries pursue decarbonization. Just as the move to steam power revolutionized shipping and logistics, the adoption of green hydrogen in ports could redefine global trade and energy value chains, leading to a greener, more sustainable future.

Morocco is actively pursuing the hydrogen economy as a strategic development opportunity and has already published several key policy documents outlining its approach, notably the Hydrogen
Roadmap in 2021 and the Morocco Offer (L’Offre) in March 2024. The green hydrogen sector is attracting private sector interest in the country. The largest green energy projects in the country are currently plans by OCP (La Tribune Afrique, 2023) to produce 1 million tons (mt) of green ammonia by 2027, rising to 3.2 mt by 2032, for its own domestic production of ammonia and fertilizer.
In addition, Morocco has received more than 40 submissions under the first round of Offre Maroc investment appraisal requests.

The European Union (EU), a key player in the hydrogen economy, plans to import up to 10 mt of green hydrogen by 2030 as part of REPowerEU and has developed policies and financing mechanisms to encourage such imports. Shipping can provide a stable source of demand for hydrogen-based green fuels.

Maritime transport accounted for 2.89 percent of global greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions in 2018
(IMO, 2020a). The key driver for the decarbonization of shipping is policy, both at the European level through the Fit For 55 package and particularly at the global level. The International Maritime
Organization (IMO) adopted an ambition to achieve net-zero GHG emissions from international shipping by or around 2050, with indicative checkpoints for reductions in absolute emissions of
20–30 percent by 2030, and 70-80 percent by 2040 relative to 2008, which is shown in Figure 1.1.

Hydrogen-based fuels from renewable electricity could offer a scalable, low-cost option for deep-sea shipping to decarbonize (World Bank, 2021b). Throughout this report these fuels are referred to as green hydrogen-based fuels. Power-to-X projects are projects that include renewable electricity generation for the purpose of producing hydrogen or a hydrogen derivative, in other words using power to produce “X”, an undefined further product.
