Where can Hydrogen be Produced,

how Cheaply, and what for?
Green hydrogen-based fuels provide a scalable alternative to fossil-based bunker fuels. Thanks to its wind and solar energy resources, Morocco has the potential to become one of the lowest levelized cost of hydrogen producers.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                6

Hydrogen may be the cleaner option to deep-sea shipping and other heavy industries, but for it to become a reality, a solid market with established producers and consumers needs to be built.
This chapter reviews existing literature on green hydrogen production potential of different countries. It describes the approach used to calculate the levelized cost of hydrogen (LCOH) to compare different value chain configurations throughout this report. It also delineates use cases in the shipping sector and the key advantages and disadvantages of the main green hydrogen-based fuels, specifically ammonia and methanol.

2.1 Global hydrogen production and trade
A global market for green hydrogen does not yet exist. Contrary to fossil fuels, liquefied natural gas, and (gray) ammonia, hydrogen is not currently shipped worldwide from one country or region to another. Approximately 90 mt of gray hydrogen is used currently per year, but production primarily occurs in or near the site of consumption (McKinsey & Company, 2024).

The general expectation is that demand and production centers for green hydrogen (and its derivatives) will emerge (IRENA, 2022b), and hydrogen will be shipped in different carriers, including ammonia and liquid organic hydrogen carriers. As part of ongoing efforts to bridge gaps between potential demand and production centers, the World Bank has been actively involved in supporting green hydrogen development in emerging markets. This includes initiatives like the Hydrogen for
Development Partnership established by the Energy Sector Management Assistance Program, and projects in South Africa, Panama, Brazil, and Mauritania, which focus on addressing technical, infrastructural, and financial barriers while promoting capacity building and knowledge sharing to accelerate the global hydrogen market (MTBS, 2024; World Bank, 2024a; World Bank, 2024b; World
Bank, 2024c). These advisory and lending projects have a specific focus on developing marine fuel bunkering hubs and sometimes support the development of a regulatory framework for hydrogen.

Demand centers are expected to be countries or regions with industrial clusters where hydrogen can play a crucial role in decarbonization as a feedstock for green hydrogen-based fuels. Production is likely to take place where costs for generating renewable electricity are lowest (possibly complemented by other sources). The rationale for this centralization of production is that the costs for renewable electricity generation constitute the majority of the LCOH (IRENA, 2020). Due to differing conditions between regions, the LCOH varies depending on location in 2030, as shown in

Figure 2.1.

Zooming in on North Africa, Figure 2.1 shows Morocco as one of the lowest LCOH producers, even in the already low-cost region of North Africa. Production costs are expected to be $1.5 to 2.5 per kg hydrogen in 2050. IRENA (2022a) estimates even lower production costs in 2050 for the region, between $0.6 and 1.3 per kg hydrogen. Morocco’s LCOH by 2050 will be slightly higher than
Australia, Canada, Chile, China, Colombia, and India but lower than all other countries and regions, including the Middle East, North and Sub-Saharan Africa.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               7

Morocco has the potential to become a major player in green hydrogen-based fuels production and distribution. This is underpinned by several characteristics (Deloitte, 2023, own assessment):

•   Low-cost production of renewable electricity.

•   Strategic geographical location at the crossroads of major maritime routes and proximity to
    Europe. This may help ports emerge as export centers.

•   Robust port infrastructure and extensive coastline.

•   Availability of space for infrastructure development.

•   Favorable investment climate.

•   Strong presence of industry that could boost demand for green hydrogen.

