Middle East and North Africa

Many countries in the Middle East and North Africa have a strategy and the potential to become a central exporting region for hydrogen. Egypt (Ministry of Environment, 2019), Algeria (Ministère de l’Energie, 2023), Tunisia (Ministère de l’Industrie, des Mines et de l’Energie, 2024) and Morocco
(Ministère de la Transition Energétique et du Développement Durable, 2021) have developed energy transition strategies, most of which have a special focus on the export of hydrogen to the EU.
In IRENA’s (2022b) estimate of hydrogen trade in 2050, exports of the North African region are equal to 35.4 mt of hydrogen equivalent (20 mt of hydrogen and 86.3 mt of ammonia). This estimate includes Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, and Egypt. These countries have published reports and announced policies stating their ambitions for hydrogen export to Europe and other regions. They are at different stages of development of hydrogen value chains.

Egypt and Morocco are at a similar stage of development, where feasibility studies have been completed for large-scale projects, and the first projects are being developed (UNIDO, 2023).
Algeria and Tunisia have set targets and developed policies but have not yet completed feasibility studies (Ghebouli, 2023; GIZ, 2024). Competition between North African countries will occur but may be softened with Europe’s projected import volumes surpassing the export volume potential of the
North African region.

European Union

Based on its renewable energy potential, the EU’s total production capacity will not be sufficient to satisfy European demand for hydrogen. The EU will, therefore, be reliant on imports
(Hydrogen4EU, 2022). The EU aims to import 10 mt of hydrogen per year by 2030 as part of the
REPowerEU initiative.5 Several EU member states, such as the Netherlands, Belgium, and Germany, will rely heavily on imports. ISPT (2024) estimates that the majority of total demand in the
Antwerp-Rotterdam-Rhine-Ruhr-Area will be imported.

Other EU countries such as France, Spain, Italy, and Portugal are expected to rely less on imports due to the favorable conditions for renewable energy production. For example, France’s hydrogen roadmap targets a capacity of 4.5 GW of electrolysis by 2030 to cover French hydrogen demand
(Ministère de l’Économie, des Finances et de la Souveraineté industrielle et numérique, 2025).
When considering hydrogen projects with preliminary offtake agreements, Spain is set to produce at least 2.5 mt per year in 2030 (Martin, 2024).

Pipeline infrastructure can be developed and constructed over time. The transmission system operators within the EU plan to have an international pipeline network starting in the 2030s
(European Hydrogen Backbone, 2023). However, even when pipelines are available for low-cost and long-distance transportation of (green) hydrogen, shipping will still be used to distribute nearly half of all hydrogen produced (IRENA, 2022b).

The reports paint a near-unanimous picture of Morocco’s production, transportation, and export potential for hydrogen and related fuels. In the instances where Morocco is not the top country, it is shown to be nearly as competitive as the top competing countries. When costs are considered, the main competition comes from countries distant from Europe, but their competitive advantage decreases due to higher transportation costs.

