2.2 The value chain for bunker fuels

2.2.1 The options for decarbonizing shipping
To reduce the shipping fleet’s carbon footprint, around 64 percent of its fuel mix is projected to be hydrogen-based by 2050 (Lloyd’s Register Maritime Decarbonisation Hub, 2023). Available options to reduce GHG emissions in maritime transport have limitations:

•   Energy efficiency measures are able to reduce energy use and therefore emissions in shipping,
    but they cannot reduce emissions all the way to zero.

•   Biofuels face limited availability, sustainability issues, and competition with other sectors
    (DNV, 2023a).

•   Liquefied natural gas offers limited GHG reduction potential (World Bank, 2021a).

•   Batteries provide insufficient range for deep-sea shipping, but may be viable for short
    shipping routes.

•   Commercial nuclear-powered vessels are not currently available and will not be for at least the
    coming decade.

•   Onboard carbon capture is technically possible, but is not expected to become popular due to its
    prohibitively high cost.

•   Fuels produced with hydrogen from renewable electricity are scarce, expensive, and have
    lower energy densities than fossil fuels. For ammonia, the engine technology is not yet mature
    (DNV, 2023a).

Despite these challenges, hydrogen-based fuels from renewable electricity could offer a scalable, low-cost option for deep-sea shipping (World Bank, 2021b). Ammonia (NH3) is a carbon-free fuel that, when produced using renewable energy, emits no carbon dioxide (CO2) during combustion.
However, it is toxic and needs specialized handling and storage infrastructure. Methanol (CH3OH), while containing carbon, can be produced sustainably from biomass or renewable hydrogen and CO2, offering a more familiar and easier-to-handle alternative with existing infrastructure for storage and transport. In particular, ammonia is seen as the cheaper option in the longer term. This is due to the difficulties in obtaining the sustainable carbon necessary for methanol production.

Unlike traditional fossil fuel-based bunker fuel, which is made by refining crude oil, green ammonia and methanol need to be produced using renewable energy and desalinated water (shown in

Figure 2.2). Hydrogen is produced using a method called electrolysis, in which water is split into
hydrogen and oxygen. The hydrogen can then be further refined into a hydrogen derivative. Ammonia is produced by combining hydrogen with nitrogen using the Haber-Bosch process, while methanol is produced by combining hydrogen with CO2.
