The electrolysis process is energy-intensive. To reduce emissions, the energy to produce hydrogen will need to come from renewable sources such as wind and solar. Only then can green hydrogen be produced. Due to the large scale of electricity required, green hydrogen projects are anticipated to be off-grid. This means the project will not be connected to the national grid and will not affect the electricity supply in the rest of the country.

Off-grid green hydrogen production projects offer the advantage of energy independence and ensuring that 100 percent of the electricity supplied is renewable. This can also be beneficial in remote areas, reducing transmission losses and ensuring energy security. However, off-grid systems may face challenges with energy storage and consistency, particularly during periods of low renewable energy generation, leading to potential interruptions in hydrogen production, or the need to install expensive batteries.
