2.2.2 Development of ship engines running on alternative fuel
Almost all ocean-going ships use internal combustion engines for propulsion and auxiliary power.
Today, methanol engines are already on the water, while hydrogen or ammonia engines for ships are gradually becoming commercially available. Several leading marine engine manufacturers have ongoing development programs to manufacture ammonia- and hydrogen-fueled combustion engines
(MAN Energy Solutions, 2023a). The first ammonia-burning two-stroke engines are expected to be installed onboard a vessel during 2025 and become commercially available in 2025 (MAN Energy
Solutions, 2023b; WinGD, 2023). The first four-stroke ammonia engine has been available since late
2023 (Wärtsilä, 2023).

From a technology maturity point of view, confidence in the uptake of hydrogen-based shipping fuels is reflected in vessel orders from shipyards. In 2025, it is estimated that 83 methanol-fueled vessels and 3 ammonia-fueled vessels will be in operation. 364 methanol-fueled vessels and 43 ammonia-
fueled vessels are on order (DNV, 2025). So-called ammonia-ready ships, that is vessels that have a design prepared for an easy switchover to run on ammonia, have recently outpaced methanol-ready ships at 322 units against 272 (Atchison, 2024).
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade              15

In terms of vessel types, order books signal that long-haul or liner shipping dominates alternatively propelled ships. While methanol orders dominate the container ship segment, ammonia-fueled ships are either bulk carriers or large gas carriers. The latter, aside from running on ammonia themselves, will carry ammonia to hydrogen import markets.

2.2.3 Jobs and socio-economic impacts of Power-to-X projects
The energy transition has the potential to foster job creation and deliver wider socio-economic benefits. Existing analyses and models of the potential for job creation are limited due to the small number of Power-to-X projects currently operational. They also vary widely – some models consider jobs only within the energy sector, some include transportation, the development of electricity and water infrastructure, and even other related sectors such as refining and chemicals (International
PtX Hub, 2024b).

Typically, green energy projects generate many jobs during the construction phase. These are unskilled or semi-skilled labor that can be trained on-the-job. These projects generate fewer operation and maintenance jobs, which last over the duration of the entire project (ESMAP, 2023).
In Power-to-X value chains, between 50–200 specific roles could be needed, depending on the size and complexity of the value chain (International PtX Hub, 2024b). Electrical engineering and industrial data processing, mechanical engineering, fluid mechanics, metrology, quality, health, safety and environment, and proficiency in English are considered key skills that may be needed in future green hydrogen value chains (France Hydrogène, 2022). The vast majority of jobs generated will be highly skilled.

Wider socio-economic benefits of Power-to-X projects include greater access to (excess) electricity generated as part of the projects, increased economic output and incomes, which may spur demand for services and economic growth. Governmental agencies will need to facilitate the regulation and implementation of Power-to-X related activities. The majority of jobs created by green energy and
Power-to-X projects may be due to the demand for these extra services, in so-called induced jobs, rather than direct project-based jobs (ESMAP, 2023). Ensuring these wider benefits requires a pro-active approach from government and municipalities. Recommendations are listed in the
Roadmap, see Chapter 8.

2.3 Conclusion
Morocco is well-placed to generate renewable electricity, hydrogen, and its derivatives due to its excellent solar and wind conditions, and advantageous location near the key offtake market of
Europe. The possible production process for green hydrogen-based bunker fuels and the different fuels’ strengths and weaknesses are provided. These characteristics inform the market, regulatory, and port assessments carried out later in the report.
Regulatory and Legal Framework

Analysis
Establishing a regulatory framework governing the use of green hydrogen-based fuels and pricing emissions are necessary conditions to supporting the nascent green hydrogen market.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                 17

A supportive regulatory and legal framework is essential for the development of Morocco as a green hydrogen-based fuels production center. Distinguishing between regulations to incentivize the production of green hydrogen-based fuels and those for handling these fuels, this chapter considers international, regional, and national rules and regulations relevant to stakeholders considering the feasibility of green hydrogen-based fuels in the maritime sector. It concludes with suggested next steps to overcome potential legal challenges related to developing green hydrogen-based fuels activities in Moroccan ports.

3.1 Regulations to incentivize the production of green hydrogen-
based bunker fuels

3.1.1 International regulations
The IMO is the United Nations’ specialized agency responsible for the safety and security of shipping and for preventing marine and atmospheric pollution by ships. Following the 2018 Initial
IMO GHG Strategy, the IMO adopted a revised strategy in 2023. In this new strategy, IMO member states agreed:

•   To reach net-zero GHG emissions from international shipping by around 2050, with interim
    checkpoints of 20–30 percent by 2030 and 70–80 percent by 2040; and

•   To make zero- or near-zero GHG energy, fuels, and technologies 5–10 percent of shipping’s
    energy mix by 2030 (IMO, 2023).

Robust policies will be required to meet the IMO’s GHG targets. In 2025, IMO Member States agreed to combine a technical measure – a pair of annual GHG fuel intensity targets – with an economic measure – a charge on each ton of emissions above the GHG fuel intensity target. These penalties are referred to as remedial units.

The measure distinguishes between Direct Compliance and Base Compliance:

•   Direct Compliance requires more ambitious GHG reductions. Vessels with emissions exceeding
    direct targets will be charged a lower Tier 1 remedial unit price which initially will be $100 per ton
    CO2eq on a well-to-wake basis.

•   Base Compliance is the minimum GHG reduction requirement. Vessels with emissions exceeding
    base levels will be charged a higher Tier 2 remedial unit price on emissions above the base levels.
    Initially, Tier 2 remedial units will be priced at $380 per ton CO2eq on a well-to-wake basis. Ships
    will also have to acquire Tier 1 remedial units for emissions above the direct compliance targets.

Vessels that overperform relative to the direct compliance target will be eligible to receive Surplus
Units. Surplus Units may be credited for a period of two years against emissions in future years or sold to vessels that do not meet the Base Compliance targets.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                                          18

Funds generated from the sale of Tier 1 and Tier 2 remedial units will be used, among other things, to reward the use of zero- or near-zero technologies, fuels, and/or energy sources, to fund different activities related to shipping’s energy transition (including maritime, coastal and port-related infrastructure) and to address potential disproportionately negative impacts on States resulting from the measure implementation (IMO, 2025a).

To ensure ships do not simply switch to fuels that generate no operational emissions, but whose production is GHG-intensive – hence merely shifting emissions to other sectors – IMO adopted
‘Guidelines on life cycle GHG intensity of marine fuels’ (LCA guidelines) in 2023 and updated them in 2024. These guidelines allow for a well-to-wake calculation, including well-to-tank and tank-to-
wake emission factors, of total GHG emissions related to the production and use of marine fuels.
Work on developing IMO’s LCA framework continues. To support this work, IMO in 2024 established a GESAMP6 Working Group on Life Cycle GHG Intensity of Marine Fuels to review scientific and technical issues (IMO, 2024b).

As an IMO member state, Morocco could monitor and contribute to developing IMO’s climate policies and the LCA guidelines.

    GESAMP (Group of Experts on the Scientific Aspects of Marine Environmental Protection) is a group of experts that, since 1969,
    has advised the United Nations system on scientific aspects of marine environmental protection.
