3.1.2 Regional regulations
Chapter 2 shows that Morocco has great potential to export hydrogen and its derivatives to
Europe, including for use by the shipping industry. Therefore, it is important to understand the EU regulations that can incentivize the demand for green hydrogen-based fuels from the EU. The two most important regulations are the EU’s Emissions Trading System (ETS) and the FuelEU Maritime
Regulation. Both are summarized below.

Since January 2024, the EU ETS has been extended to cover GHG emissions from ships of 5,000 gross tonnage and above entering EU ports, regardless of the flag they fly. The system covers
100 percent of emissions occurring between two and within EU ports and 50 percent of emissions on voyages into or out of the EU. By defining and gradually reducing the maximum amount of
GHG that can be emitted, the system aims to incentivize energy efficiency, low-carbon solutions, and reductions of the price difference between low-/zero-carbon and traditional maritime fuels
(European Commission, 2024b).

To reduce the risk of evasion by container ships and of relocation of container transshipment activities outside the European Economic Area, certain ports outside the EU have been designated as
‘neighboring container transshipment ports’. This means that stops by container ships at these ports do not count to determine the start or the end of a voyage covered by the EU ETS.

To be identified as a ‘neighboring container transshipment port’, three criteria need to be met:

•   The port’s share of container transshipment exceeds 65 percent of its total container traffic
    during the most recent 12-month period for which data is available

•   It is located outside the EU but less than 300 nautical miles from an EU port

•   The country of the port does not effectively apply measures equivalent to the ETS for that port.

In October 2023, the European Commission identified Tanger Med and East Port Said (Egypt) as
‘neighboring container transshipment ports’ (European Commission, 2024a). This means that the EU
ETS requirements are relevant to Tanger Med and systems may need to be put in place to comply with all requirements. It also means that demand for green hydrogen-based bunker fuels in Tanger
Med will likely arise and increase, making green hydrogen-based bunker fuel provision a high priority for the port. In the future, Nador West Med might also be covered under the EU ETS.

While the EU ETS focuses on reducing total emissions, another EU regulation – the FuelEU Maritime
Regulation, adopted as part of the Fit for 55 regulatory package – addresses the fuel type and GHG intensity. FuelEU Maritime sets well-to-wake GHG emission intensity requirements on energy used on board ships over 5,000 gross tonnage trading in the EU and has been in force since 1 January
2025. There are different compliance options, including the use of RFNBOs (such as e-methanol and
e-ammonia), which must be certified according to the EU Renewable Energy Directive (DNV, 2024b;
European Commission, 2024b; Mærsk Mc-Kinney Møller Center for Zero Carbon Shipping, 2024a;
Mærsk Mc-Kinney Møller Center for Zero Carbon Shipping, 2024b). For more information on the certification requirements, please see Box 2.1.
