3.1.3 National regulations
Morocco published a National Hydrogen Roadmap in 2021, stating that 140 billion to
1,000 billion dirhams of investments are necessary by 2050 (Ministère de la Transition Energétique et du Développement Durable, 2021). The country also launched the Cluster Green H2 in 2021, which brings together more than 80 organizations and companies to help develop a green hydrogen ecosystem.

Most recently, the government published the Moroccan Offer, L’Offre Maroc, which indicates the conditions under which green hydrogen projects will be permitted to proceed. It is designed to attract national and international investors interested in green hydrogen production by providing an operational and incentive strategy covering the entire value chain of this promising sector
(Royaume du Maroc, 2024).

The Offre Maroc consists of six parts, which are summarized as follows:

1.   Scope: The Offre Maroc is aimed at investors wishing to produce green hydrogen at an industrial
     scale in Morocco for the domestic market and/or export. It encompasses upstream (generating
     electricity from renewable sources and electrolysis) and downstream (transforming green
     hydrogen into derivative products) activities, with incentives for those targeting only certain
     parts of the value chain.

2. Land available for developing green hydrogen: The government has identified significant public
   land of approximately 1 million hectares that is dedicated, accessible, and has a high potential
   for green hydrogen production. A phased approach will be used to adapt to developments in the
   sector. In the first phase, 300,000 hectares will be made available.

3. Necessary infrastructure for developing the green hydrogen sector: The Offre Maroc
   provides for competitive infrastructure in line with international standards, with different
   ongoing feasibility studies on port infrastructure, hydrogen and gas pipeline networks,
   water desalination, hydrogen storage, and integration with the local industry.

4. Incentives for green hydrogen investments: These include investment incentives, such as
   support schemes in line with the new investment charter, and tax and customs incentives,
   including exemptions from import duties and Value Added Tax for goods acquired domestically
   or upon importation. Additionally, industrial acceleration zones could be developed for the green
   hydrogen industrial ecosystem, allowing investors to benefit from tax and customs advantages.

5. Process of selecting investors and contracting with the state: Investors interested in green
   hydrogen projects are invited to submit their offers to the Moroccan Agency for Sustainable
   Energy (Masen), the focal point organization of the Offre Maroc. The first round of submissions
   included over 40 expressions of interest (Ministère de l’Investissement, de la Convergence et
   de l’Evaluation des Politiques Publiques, 2024). The projects will be evaluated according to
   various criteria, such as the investors’ financial robustness, experience in the green hydrogen
   sector, and positive impact on Morocco. The initial negotiations with the selected investors will
   notably concern the allocation of land, possibly leading to the conclusion of a preliminary land
   reservation contract.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                 21

6. Governance of the green hydrogen sector: Masen plays a key role as a focal point for investors,
   ensuring coordination and providing support throughout the process. A Steering Committee
   and a Green Hydrogen Investment Committee supervise the implementation of the Offre
   Maroc, preselecting investors, coordinating actions, and proposing recommendations to the
   government. These committees meet regularly to monitor progress and adjust strategies as
   needed (Royaume du Maroc, 2024).

3.2 Regulations for handling green hydrogen-based bunker fuels

3.2.1 International regulations
Due to the urgency of guiding countries, shipowners, and the shipping industry on the safe use of alternative fuels and to support the achievement of its emission targets, IMO is developing guidelines for the safety of ships using alternative fuels, including ammonia, methanol, and hydrogen.

In 2020, IMO adopted ‘Interim Guidelines for the Safety of Ships Using Methyl/Ethyl Alcohol as
Fuel’, aiming to provide an international standard for ships using such fuels. The guidelines include provisions for the arrangement, installation, control and monitoring of machinery, equipment and systems using methyl/ethyl alcohol as fuel to minimize the risk to the ship, its crew, and the environment (IMO, 2020b). IMO plans to finalize the revision of these interim guidelines in
2027, and to develop mandatory safety standards for ships using methyl/ethyl alcohols as fuel
(as opposed to non-mandatory guidelines) (IMO, 2024d).

In addition, IMO has approved interim guidelines for the safety of ships using ammonia as fuel and is considering revising them over the next two years (IMO, 2024d; IMO, 2025b). The organization is also working on interim guidelines for the safety of ships using hydrogen as fuel (IMO, 2024d).

Furthermore, interim guidelines for ships using fuel cells have been adopted. Their goal is to ensure that fuel cell power installations are set up and installed as safely and reliably as traditional oil-fueled machinery, no matter the type of fuel cell or fuel used. Depending on the fuel used, other regulations and provisions, such as the interim guidelines for using methanol as fuel, apply in addition to these guidelines (IMO, 2022). IMO is considering the development of mandatory safety standards for ships using fuels cells over the next few years (IMO, 2024d).

3.2.2 National regulations
Port Law No. 15-02 constitutes the fundamental legal framework governing the organization, development, and management of Moroccan ports. This law establishes the legal framework governing port activities in Morocco, including commercial activities such as bunkering.

Commercial activities are subject to authorization. This authorization could involve compliance with certain conditions regarding safety, environment, and fuel quality, as well as the payment of port fees or other costs associated with this activity. This law applies to bunkering and the storage and handling of fuels in the port since it involves a terminal concession and the operation of a terminal.
Law 15-02 also manages the temporary land occupation for storage spaces.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                  22

In addition to the Port Law, the Port Operations Regulation establishes specific procedures for port activities, including storage activities and ship bunkering. Each port’s operating regulations define the requirements for safety, security, and environmental protection and the operating modes of specific installations.

These operating regulations apply to the Moroccan ports of Tanger Med, Mohammedia, Jorf Lasfar, and the new site at Tan-Tan, each with its own regulatory requirements and procedures.
However, no regulatory texts in Morocco specifically govern the supply of ship bunkers with green hydrogen-based fuels.

It is advised that national guidelines on safety related to handling and storage of green hydrogen-
based fuels be outlined to ensure a level playing field and knowledge sharing. These should be based on international standards and best practices. Please see the elaboration of the approach applied in the Netherlands (Appendix A7) as an example for developing this type of regulation in Morocco.

3.3 Next steps
To overcome potential legal challenges related to the development of green hydrogen-based fuels activities in Moroccan ports, the following strategies can be considered:

1.   In-depth analysis of the regulatory framework: Carry out a benchmark and in-depth analysis
     of the existing or planned regulatory framework to identify gaps and inconsistencies that could
     hinder the development of green hydrogen-based fuels activities. This analysis will be important
     due to the toxicity of some green hydrogen-based fuels, the water needed for producing
     green hydrogen-based fuels, and the effluents generated in this process. It will determine the
     adjustments needed to ensure compatibility and consistency with sustainability objectives and
     international standards.

2. Dialogue with authorities: Engage in a constructive dialogue with the relevant regulatory
   authorities, including ministries, port agencies, and regulatory bodies, to discuss potential legal
   challenges and explore mutually acceptable solutions. Working closely with policymakers and
   key stakeholders will facilitate the adoption of appropriate regulatory measures.

3. Stakeholder consultation: Actively involve stakeholders, including companies, industry
   associations, environmental groups, and civil society organizations, in identifying and resolving
   legal challenges. Stakeholder consultation will ensure that proposed regulations consider all
   stakeholders’ perspectives and interests.

4. Training and raising awareness: Organize training sessions and awareness-raising campaigns
   to inform stakeholders about the legal and regulatory requirements for green hydrogen-based
   fuels activities. A better understanding of the regulations in force will help to improve compliance
   and reduce the risk of infringements or legal disputes.

5. Ongoing monitoring: Establish an ongoing monitoring mechanism to track changes in
   the regulatory framework and identify new opportunities or challenges that may emerge.
   This monitoring will help adjust strategies and practices per ongoing legal and regulatory
   developments.
Market Analysis

Among the four Moroccan ports selected in this study, Tanger Med is the one that has the most potential as a green fuel bunkering hub, competition from other African and European ports notwithstanding. Domestic demand is expected to emerge, too, and here the other three ports will come into play.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               24

The maritime sector can catalyze the hydrogen economy both as a consumer and an enabler.
Deep- sea shipping, being globally regulated, can provide a stable demand for green hydrogen.
This chapter provides an understanding of the future market dynamics for green hydrogen, ammonia, and methanol, and the potential demand for these commodities produced in Morocco.

The demand for hydrogen may come from three sources: Moroccan ports, export markets, and domestic industry. Each of the three demand subchapters includes an assessment of the risks and opportunities for green hydrogen and its derivatives in Morocco.

4.1 The possibility to serve bunker demand at the four
Moroccan ports
It is necessary to estimate the future bunker fuel demand in Morocco in order to identify the scale of production needed to satisfy this demand. The future bunker potential is determined in a three-step process:

1.   Forecasting the evolution of the world fleet’s fuel requirements.

2. Forecasting bunker requirements for different ports.

3. Combining these analyses to assess the fuel-specific bunker demand.

The actual demand for green methanol and green ammonia in Morocco will be determined by a complex interplay of factors, including evolving policies and regulations in the EU and other jurisdictions and global policies laid out by the IMO. Other factors include macroeconomic growth and specific growth rates in the shipping industry, which would influence total fuel demand.
Changes in capital markets, such as interest rates, influence the costs of developing value chains for green hydrogen-based fuels.

A description of the methodology and an overview of the assumptions applied in the analysis are included in Appendix A2. To accommodate uncertainty, varying key assumptions are used to create low, medium, and high demand scenarios to investigate the difference between a minimal and an aggressive transition. Green hydrogen-based fuels are at the very beginning of the anticipated
S-curve growth in uptake. Hence, demand starts at lower values leading up to 2030 and then rapidly increases through to 2050.

The unique shapes of fuel uptake are fundamentally rooted in several critical factors influencing the adoption dynamics of these fuels. The fragmented understanding of the cost of capital for green hydrogen introduces significant market and technological uncertainty, which can hinder investment decisions in hydrogen-based fuels like methanol and ammonia and constrain the development of the necessary infrastructure. The future trajectories of both methanol and ammonia are also heavily influenced by prices, regulatory frameworks, technological innovations, and the establishment of essential infrastructure. This landscape creates conditions conducive to exponential growth, particularly in high adoption scenarios, as countries face additional pressure to address climate change, but also demonstrate strong economic growth to fuel this increasing demand. This is reflected in the demand scenarios shown below: the uptake is initially slow and then grows significantly from 2030 onwards.
