Given the low volumes for each port on the Atlantic coast of Morocco, it is unlikely that each of these ports will develop bunkering facilities for green hydrogen-based fuels. However, on an aggregate level, there is a significant potential bunker market, especially when a portion of the North-South passing traffic can be served. Depending on the ports’ ambitions, the vessel routes, connecting bunker hubs along those routes, and other factors, some of the aggregated demand could be supplied from a central bunker hub along the Atlantic coast.

4.1.4 Risks and opportunities
When combining bunkering demand from Tanger Med, the Atlantic coast ports included in the study, and passing North-South traffic, demand (in hydrogen equivalent terms) could be 0.2 mt per year in
2030, rising to 2.83 mt per year in 2050. Demand at Tanger Med is higher than at all the other ports combined. Where Tanger Med could supply up to 1 mtpa of green fuels by 2030 (medium scenario), the demand at Atlantic ports between Mohammedia and Tan-Tan would amount to 0.017 mtpa for domestic bunkering and 0.173 mtpa due to passing traffic on the Atlantic coast.

Moroccan ports, particularly Tanger Med, will face competition from ports in Spain and Egypt to supply green hydrogen-based fuels. However, based on their levelized cost of electricity, they are not likely to be able to offer these fuels at lower costs than Morocco. Also, in an emerging and supply-
constrained market, increasing the availability of green hydrogen-based fuels reduces risks for shipping liners. It increases their flexibility in routing, thereby growing and developing the market and increasing the global uptake of green hydrogen-based fuels. During the transition period, competition can be both a threat and a strength.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               29

Some bunker ports in other countries can also be possible off-takers when there is insufficient local production capacity. The port of Las Palmas could be an example of such a port. Due to its strategic position in the North-South Atlantic shipping corridor, it currently supplies 2.25 mtpa of bunker fuels
(Integr8, 2023). It can supply ships of any type or size because it can bunker at berth all year and at anchorage when ocean swells are below 2 meters.

As mentioned at the start of this section, IMO policy on decarbonizing shipping is being developed.
Ensuring that the Moroccan government remains well informed on these ongoing discussions and negotiations is essential to accurately assess the direction and speed of the market for green hydrogen-based fuels. This enables adequate decision-making on infrastructure development.

4.2 The possibility of exporting hydrogen as a commodity
Morocco has a solid opportunity to become a significant production and export center of green hydrogen. It has prime conditions for low-cost renewable energy production, is located near one of the world’s largest demand centers, the EU, which aims to import 10 mt of hydrogen by 2030
(see Section 2.1), has multiple ports open to international trade, and has thousands of kilometers of coastline, which provide ample opportunities for infrastructure to facilitate exports.

The Morocco Green Hydrogen Roadmap (Ministère de la Transition Energétique et du Développement
Durable, 2021) projects a total hydrogen export potential of 3.4–6.9 mtpa by 2050. Deloitte (2023)
