Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade              31

The Mission Possible Partnership (2022) investigated the potential of ammonia in industrial and maritime applications and how the transition of ammonia can be facilitated. The report considers ammonia the primary option to power 55 percent to 90 percent of the shipping sector, requiring 295 to 670 mt of ammonia in 2050. Considering that total ammonia production is expected to range between 580 and 830 mtpa, according to the same source, bunkering will be a significant end-use of exported hydrogen and ammonia.

Verschuur et al. (2024) developed a spatial modeling framework to quantify the cost-optimal fuel supply to decarbonize shipping in 2050 using green ammonia. They find that the supply of green hydrogen-based fuels will cluster in a few large supply clusters, which will serve regional demand clusters with very limited long-distance ammonia transport.

Rotterdam is Europe’s leading bunkering hub, with an annual bunkering volume of around 8 mt, mainly fossil fuels. It has ambitions to decarbonize its port operations and produce green hydrogen-
based fuels alternatives, including ammonia. Still, the production volume will be insufficient to satisfy demand in the port and the Antwerp-Rotterdam-Rhine-Ruhr hinterland (ISPT, 2024).

Antwerp is Europe’s second-largest bunkering hub after Rotterdam, with an annual bunkering volume of around 6 mt, again mainly fossil fuels. The port of Hamburg too has ambitions to become a green hydrogen hub.

Algeciras is the main bunkering port of the Western Mediterranean, providing around 3.8 mtpa of fuel. The proximity of Algeciras to Morocco presents export opportunities, although Spain aims to become a large producer of hydrogen.

Gibraltar is the second largest bunkering port of the Western Mediterranean, with a volume of
3.4 mtpa. Like Algeciras, Gibraltar provides an excellent opportunity to export hydrogen due to its proximity to Morocco. Gibraltar does not have the hinterland that Algeciras has to produce hydrogen potentially, but it could also be supplied from Spain.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                32

For long-distance shipping between hubs such as Singapore, Shanghai, Rotterdam, Tanger Med, or Los Angeles Long Beach, it is necessary for green hydrogen-based fuels to be available at these hubs and along the route between them. This presents an opportunity for the green hydrogen-based fuels produced in Morocco to be exported to these bunkering hubs. Although transportation costs of these fuels constitute a small part of the total costs, green hydrogen-based fuels will most likely be exported from Morocco to European bunkering hubs such as Rotterdam, Antwerp, Gibraltar, and Algeciras. Nevertheless, other options may emerge in the longer term, and the network of bunkering hubs evolves with a changing fuel mix used by the global shipping fleet.

4.2.2 Exporting hydrogen for the aviation industry
The maritime sector is not the only hard-to-abate transport sector. The aviation sector is also looking at ways to decarbonize its energy demand. Flying electric or on hydrogen will likely become an option for short-haul flights, but other sustainable alternatives are required for long-haul flights.
The most likely option is Sustainable Aviation Fuels (SAFs). There are two main categories of SAFs:
biofuels and synthetic fuels.

The Moroccan aviation market is interesting, but the EU aviation sector has the most significant potential for Morocco. In 2025, the ReFuel EU legislation entered into force. The EU mandates a step-by-step SAF adoption, with additional targets for synthetic SAFs (eSAFs). These targets are presented in Figure 4.6. The EU will require 14.8 mtpa of SAF by 2040 and 28.6 mtpa by 2050 (EASA,
2022). Half of this demand must be eSAF and the EU cannot currently produce these amounts.

