SAFs are a convenient solution. Their chemical and physical characteristics are almost identical to conventional fuels, and they can be used as drop-in fuels. Adopting SAFs does not require retrofitting or building new planes.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               33

Currently, most SAFs are biofuels produced with oils, waste fats, and greases as feedstock using the hydrotreated esters and fatty acids pathway. Gray hydrogen is one of the main building blocks in this production pathway. Replacing this gray hydrogen with green hydrogen further reduces the carbon footprint, ensuring future acceptance and partly fulfilling compliance with the eSAF blend requirement. While this is currently the only commercially viable pathway for SAF production, it is difficult to scale up due to the decentralized feedstock availability and the lack of total feedstock volumes. The alcohol-to-jet SAF pathway is gaining momentum, with several companies announcing significant production capacity. Other biological feedstock options are also available, but to classify them as sustainable, they must not compete with food production or harm the environment.

The synthetic power-to-liquid pathway theoretically has a limitless supply, as the only resources required for production are electricity, water, and carbon. The carbon must be supplied from a sustainable source (as discussed in Section 2.2.1).

The main reason that eSAFs are not used widely is due to the cost, as Jet A fuel is forecast to remain cheaper until 2050 (ICCT, 2022). Currently, there is no financial incentive to switch to eSAFs.
Despite the price difference, the ReFuelEU and similar regulations in other countries will force the sector to adopt (e)SAFs.

Morocco is well positioned to produce eSAFs at a lower price than the EU due to the abundance of renewable energy and available space. The ReFuelEU legislation guarantees market demand nearby, which de-risks the investments required to establish production capacity.

Morocco can leverage its hydrogen production capacity in three roles for SAF production, by becoming a:

•   Blending country: Produce eSAFs locally and blend them with conventional fuels. Export these
    fuels as aviation-ready fuels.

•   eSAF producing country: Produce eSAFS or hydrogen locally and export them to a country taking
    up the blending role, as Spain does.

•   Transit and distribution country: Morocco can import feedstock from South America or Africa
    and use locally produced green hydrogen for SAF production.

4.2.3 Risks and opportunities
Export market size and timing will depend on the development of regulations in the EU and on the global level. Monitoring these regulations may help prepare Morocco for the risks and opportunities these export markets present. This includes:

•   The EU’s Fit for 55 package is an ambitious program that outlines the objectives and criteria
    for producing, importing, and using renewable hydrogen. This also includes the aviation sector,
    which could be an additional potential export market. The political environment in which the
    European Commission operates is subject to constant change and so is policy. The programs to
    build the required infrastructure for import and transmission and the funds to cover the viability
    gap of renewable hydrogen will be instrumental in delivering the EU’s goals.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade               34

•   The global market for renewable hydrogen has been impacted by recent macroeconomic
    developments in terms of inflation and costs of capital. Costs of labor, materials, renewable
    energy, and shortages in the value chains have increased recently. Yet, the long-term outlook
    remains positive, with significant cost reductions still anticipated until 2050 (Hydrogen
    Council, 2023). These developments are of crucial importance for the viability of renewable
    hydrogen globally.

•   IMO policy development, particularly regarding fuel standards, economic instruments
    pricing GHG emissions, guidelines on lifecycle GHG intensity of marine fuels as well as fuel
    handling regulations, discussed further in Section 3.1.1 and Section 3.2.1. More stringent rules
    regarding GHG emissions would speed up the uptake of green hydrogen-based bunker fuels
    produced in line with the IMO guidelines on lifecycle GHG intensity of marine fuels. Here, particular
    attention should be paid to the definition of admissible carbon sources for methanol production as
    it would likely influence the viability of methanol applications in shipping.

4.3 The possibility of using hydrogen in domestic industry
Green hydrogen is gaining attention as a sustainable and clean energy source and various industries are exploring its potential applications. Industries that may particularly benefit from green hydrogen include:

•   Power plants: Because of the expected connection of renewable energy to the grid, the power
    plants will, in future, only run at low wind and solar hours. For these limited hours (1,000–2,000
    a year), hydrogen could replace natural gas to avoid any CO2 emissions in the electricity system.
    Power plants running on natural gas are easier to convert or switch to co-firing systems. Hence,
    certain power plants in Morocco were prioritized in our assessment.

•   Chemical industry: Hydrogen is used as feedstock for producing ammonia, a key component
    in fertilizers.

•   Steel production: Hydrogen can replace traditional fossil fuels in the steel manufacturing
    process, reducing carbon emissions.

•   Refining: Gray hydrogen is used in refining processes. The shift to green hydrogen helps reduce
    direct carbon emissions.

•   Other energy-intensive industries: Energy-intensive industries, such as cement, glass, plastic,
    and paper, use high temperatures in production currently fed by fossil fuels. Green hydrogen
    could replace these fossil fuels to produce low-carbon high temperatures.

•   Fuel production for mobility: Hydrogen can be used as a clean fuel for aircraft or vessels,
    reducing emissions from the aviation and shipping industries.

The adoption of green hydrogen varies across industries and is expected to be crucial in achieving decarbonization goals globally. The feasibility of its implementation depends on factors such as local infrastructure, policy support, and economic considerations.
