5.2 Spatial deep-dives for handling hydrogen derivatives
This section delves into the spatial dynamics of establishing a complete hydrogen value chain around the port, crucial for supporting the transition to green shipping fuels. By mapping out the entire value chain8 —from hydrogen production and storage to distribution and bunkering—this analysis provides a comprehensive understanding of the logistical and infrastructural requirements.
This detailed examination is essential to identify optimal locations for new infrastructure, minimize transportation costs, and ensure efficient integration with the port’s activities. The insights gained will inform strategic planning and investment decisions, fostering the development of a robust and sustainable hydrogen economy around the port.

First, an overview of the hydrogen chain is provided with spatial and safety considerations for each port. This is followed by a list of possible spatial set-ups for each port.

To identify feasible solutions for hydrogen production at ports, the complete hydrogen chain is considered when determining the spatial footprints for bunkering and exporting hydrogen or hydrogen carriers (see Figure 5.5). Feasible solutions are ones that could be implemented based on this first assessment, but there may be further limitations out of the scope of this report. This includes land surface area, space for the required safety zones, and space at berths and in the port basin.

The footprints for methanol and ammonia are in the same order of magnitude (production and handling). All solutions for the ports are for off-grid hydrogen production. This approach is beneficial in remote areas, reducing transmission losses, ensuring energy supply, and sustainability of the electricity supplied. In order to ensure a stable supply of electricity, projects either have to use energy storage (batteries), or oversize renewable energy generation installations in order to comply with the minimum load requirement for electrolysis and ammonia and methanol production.

    Unlike traditional fossil-based bunker fuel, which is made by refining crude oil, green ammonia and methanol need to be produced
    using renewable energy and desalinated water. Green hydrogen is produced using a method called electrolysis, in which water
    is split into hydrogen and oxygen. The hydrogen can then be further refined into a hydrogen derivative. Ammonia is produced
    by combining hydrogen with nitrogen using the Haber-Bosch process, while methanol is produced by combining hydrogen with
    CO2. While carbon-neutral hydrogen can also be produced by breaking down natural gas and capturing the carbon by-product,
    Morocco does not possess large reserves of methane and would need to import it to produce blue hydrogen. Blue hydrogen is
    therefore not considered in this analysis.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                  53

The value chain includes:

•   Energy generation: Generating green energy with sources like wind and solar.

•   Hydrogen and hydrogen carrier production: Desalination of water, electrolysis of hydrogen,
    further processing of hydrogen into other carriers such as ammonia.

•   Transport of hydrogen carrier from production to storage.

•   Storage of hydrogen carriers.

•   Distribution of hydrogen carriers from jetties to ships or via pipeline.

•   End users: End use and processing methods and operations required. This study is limited to the
    following end users:

    –    Bunkering of vessels in the port.
    –    Cabotage/Import/Export: Hydrogen carriers are transported by vessels.
    –    Local demand for green hydrogen and derivatives.

