The following facilities are considered:

•    Electrolyzer: The electrolyzer requires fresh water from the desalination plant and electricity.
     The facility produces the hydrogen necessary for ammonia production. The hydrogen will be
     transported by pipeline to the ammonia production facility.

•    Ammonia production facility: It requires the hydrogen produced from the electrolyzer and
     electricity. The air separation unit must supply the nitrogen needed to produce ammonia. In most
     cases, this unit is put on the plot of the production unit, and the size of the unit is negligible
     compared to the size of the production facility. Therefore, throughout this study the unit is
     combined with the production facility in the spatial implementation. The Haber-Bosch process,
     which produces ammonia, requires a constant cooling water supply. This does not have to be
     desalinated water and can be supplied from the sea. The warmed-up cooling water can also be
     discharged at sea. However, it is important to separate the inlet and outlet from each other to
     ensure the warmed-up water is not pumped back in as cooling water. The produced ammonia
     must be stored in tanks. The product is transported to storage through a pipeline.

•    Ammonia storage: The storage of ammonia is done in isolated tanks with a cooling system.
     This system requires electricity. From these tanks, the ammonia is either pumped into the
     bunker vessel or the export vessel by loading arms and a loading pipeline.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                   55

•   Pipelines:

    –   Hydrogen pipeline: Hydrogen will be transported in gas form. When transporting hydrogen,
        attention must be paid to leaks as hydrogen molecules are very small and can leak through
        materials more easily than other gases. Evaporation rates are so small that they do not pose
        any danger and can be emitted in the air. Hydrogen pipelines are considered safe and often
        existing gas pipelines can be repurposed.
    –   Ammonia production pipeline: A constant volume of ammonia is pumped from the production
        facility to the storage tanks through this cooled pipeline. In this way, a constant volume can
        be produced and the pipelines will remain cooled. A regular-sized pipeline can do this, as no
        high flow rate is needed.
    –   Ammonia loading pipeline: This pipeline will supply the bunker or export vessel. It is
        important to minimize the fueling time as vessels laying at berth are cost- and space-
        intensive. Therefore, pipelines with a large diameter are required to supply an instant high
        flow rate when the vessel is berthed. These pipelines are expensive, so placing the storage
        facility as close as possible to the offloading facility is important to minimize the pipeline
        length and, thus, the costs.

•   Some facilities are already in place in some ports or outside the port area. These facilities will,
    therefore, not be included in the spatial implementation plans in this report but should not be
    neglected. They are:

    –   Desalination plant: This plant is already constructed or planned in some ports. It should
        preferably be close to the electrolyzer since the latter requires high volumes of clean water.
    –   Electricity connection point: This point is already in the ports. Per port, the substations
        are inventoried and, where known, considered when deciding the location of the facilities
        requiring energy. These points are assumed to be fixed and, therefore, not considered for
        spatial planning purposes.
    –   Sea: It is needed to supply cooling water for production facilities. The inlet and outlet
        points are flexible and do not require spatial planning. However, it is recommended that the
        facilities be placed close to shore to minimize pipeline lengths.
    –   Offloading facility/berths: The offloading facility always requires berths at a fixed location
        in the ports. The offloading facility can be combined with an existing berth, or a new berth
        can be constructed near the existing berths, for example, on the inside of a breakwater.

5.2.3 Safety impact and safety zones for the facilities
Hydrogen and its derivatives are hazardous substances. They are flammable, toxic, and reactive.
The risks are typically mitigated by establishing dedicated safety areas around the facilities, reducing the probability of negative effects in surroundings, including concurrent industries and public facilities.

Since handling these substances in ports is not yet conventional, no international guidelines and standards have been established regarding acceptable risks and corresponding safety areas.
The first step in determining the required safety areas is stating the risks of the substances and identifying existing safety guidelines. Subsequently, based on expert knowledge and benchmark
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                   56

experience, safety areas relevant to different port activities are estimated. These estimated values provide a first insight but require further study in subsequent project stages when more process, throughput, and layout information become available. A detailed assessment of the safety risks for each part of the hydrogen value chain is available in Appendices A7 and A8.

5.3 Detailed mapping for each port
An analysis of the possible practical application of hydrogen-based fuels was conducted.
The footprints of the facilities have been compared with the port master plans, and optional spatial planning has been identified to assess whether all elements can be accommodated inside the port.
However, a detailed site selection and masterplan study needs to be conducted for each port at the next stage.

For each port, suitable locations for the electrolyzer and the ammonia or methanol production plants were identified. Suitable locations were defined as locations without currently competing activities, but their suitability was not evaluated further. In addition, storage space within each port was identified for storing the ammonia or methanol, as close to the bunkering berth as possible.
Berth placement was also evaluated.

Tanger Med has four different options to bunker green fuels, and each correspond to one of two sets of electrolyzer and green fuel production areas. Mohammedia has one possible option for electrolyzers and ammonia. Two options for electrolyzer and ammonia were identified for
Jorf Lasfar. Within the port, there should be sufficient space to accommodate bunkering vessels at existing berths, but space was also identified for a possible new berth.

5.3.1 Tanger Med port
The spatial implementation in Tanger Med is focused on berth placement and on exploring the possible space for bunkering operations. This is because space is extremely valuable and in high demand, making it more difficult to find space for new activities within the port. These are the following identified berthing arrangements (indicated in Figure 5.7):

•   Option 0: Use of existing berths in the hydrocarbon terminal (close to Option 2 in Figure 5.7).

•   Option 1: On the existing breakwater of the container terminal.

•   Option 2: On the existing breakwater of the hydrocarbon terminal.

•   Option 3.1 and 3.2: Offshore.

For each bunkering option, a set of suitable areas for electrolyzer and ammonia production were also identified (electrolyzer in red on the right, ammonia in blue on the left). It must be noted that fuel production in the vicinity of Tanger Med is highly unlikely. Identifying alternative locations for ammonia or methanol synthesis would be preferable.
