5.4 Conclusion
The adoption of green shipping fuels hinges on the ability to produce and distribute hydrogen efficiently and sustainably. Ports with robust infrastructure, strategic planning, and proximity to renewable energy sources are well-positioned to become leaders in this transformation.
By examining the existing infrastructure, spatial dynamics, and future plans of key ports, this report has identified the critical factors that will drive the successful integration of these green fuels into maritime activities.

Tanger Med is well suited to becoming a bunkering hub for green fuels. Due to possible spatial limitations within the port, ship-to-ship bunkering and production of fuels outside the port are most feasible. Four options for bunkering green fuels were identified. Mohammedia has a distinct advantage due to its proximity to salt caverns, which might make it interesting for hydrogen storage. Jorf Lasfar is well positioned to handle hydrogen and ammonia, due to existing infrastructure and local industry. Tan-Tan is promising for local production and export, due to great solar and wind power, as well as sufficient space.
