6.2 Assumptions
A cost model was used to determine the optimal cost of each scenario. This section provides an overview of the assumptions used in the model. The costs of each scenario are calculated through an Earnings Before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation, and Amortization-based business case model.
The results are shown in the levelized cost, showing the average cost per unit. See Appendix A6 for an explanation of the model and value chain assumptions.

Relevant assumptions:

•      Pipeline distance calculation: The distances that products must travel through pipes are
       measured using straight lines, avoiding water bodies and mountains, with an additional
       25 percent margin added to account for required deviations.

•      Bidirectional pipelines: We assume that the pipelines are bidirectional. This means only one
       pipeline is required if the same route is used to transport hydrogen to and from the storage
       facility. Furthermore, the pipelines are assumed to handle 50 percent of the annual production
       throughput because the Haber-Bosch process for ammonia production will consume the other
       50 percent.

•      Tan-Tan port construction: Since no suitable port is available around Tan-Tan, the scenarios
       involving shipping from Tan-Tan assume a new port will be constructed. This cost is considered.

•      Export-related costs: The calculation assumes free-on-board costs. Hence, the costs of
       exporting to another country either via shipping or pipeline, are not considered. Only transport
       costs between Moroccan ports have been considered.

•      Value chain optimization assumptions: The cost of solar energy generation is assumed at
       €900 per kW, while that of wind energy generation is at €1,300 per kW (IRENA, 2022a).12
       We assume specific annual full load hours for solar and wind energy per location. We used a
       tool to calculate the optimal capacities for solar and wind (as a percentage of total installed
       renewable energy sources), electrolyzer capacity, and hydrogen derivative production capacity.
       The curtailment of renewable energy represents the energy that cannot be used by the
       electrolyzer and is a result of financial optimization. We keep the hydrogen storage capacity
       constant at 10 hours’ worth of hydrogen production.

•      Assumptions for capital and operational expenditures: The renewable energy costs shown in
       this chapter represent the solar and wind energy used for hydrogen production. The electrolyzer
       costs include both its initial capital investment and ongoing maintenance. The electrolyzer
       capacity is optimally scaled on the available solar and wind hours. Therefore, the capital
       expenditures (CAPEX) of the electrolyzer differs according to the location-specific availability
       of wind and solar. For example, if there are fewer solar and wind hours you need a larger
       electrolyzer to produce the desired volumes. The pipeline and storage costs represent CAPEX,
       maintenance expenses, and the energy required for operation.

•      Electrical power generation and consumption proportion assumptions for the four locations:
       All scenarios are based on off-grid configurations (see Table 6.2).

