6.3 Preferred location for hydrogen production

6.3.1 Calculation of LCOH
To compare the costs of producing green hydrogen-based fuels, the LCOH has been calculated at different sites near the four selected ports. These sites are within 150 km of the select ports.
They were chosen for two reasons: First, transporting the equipment necessary for green energy and green hydrogen production is logistically challenging and often requires specialized ports and specific access roads. Second, a location close to a body of water is desirable to limit the distance between water and the electrolyzer plant and the electricity production sites.

The estimated production cost of ammonia has been assessed. The costs are indicative and can be used for comparison at this stage, as they are calculated similarly for all locations.13 In each of the areas, a specific renewable energy site is selected that is able to cover:

•      100 and 500 ktpa of green hydrogen production.

•      Which equals 560–2,800 ktpa of ammonia.

•      Which equals 790–3,950 ktpa of methanol.

For each site, solar panels and onshore wind turbines are considered as electricity sources.

The full load hours of solar and wind per location greatly influence the cost differences
(see Table 6.2). This is because the majority of hydrogen production cost comes from electricity cost.
When considering methanol as a vector, the production costs are in the same magnitude (depending on CO2 prices) as ammonia production costs, as most of the value chain cost breakdown (renewable electricity generation, hydrogen production, storage, and transport) remains the same.

