6.3.3 Comparison with existing LCOH calculations
In comparing our LCOH estimates to those found in the literature, several key factors contribute to our higher calculated values. One significant difference is that our LCOH includes the full hydrogen value chain, whereas earlier estimates, commonly discussed in conferences or the media, often focused solely on hydrogen production and used grid connections to reduce the impact of intermittent renewable energy supply.

The LCOH estimates are for 2022–23. Given that projects intended for completion in 2030 need to account for financial and logistical planning several years in advance (by 2025), using present-
day figures offers a more practical view of costs than projecting costs to 2030. The current cost landscape shows that while solar energy prices continue to decrease, wind energy costs have unexpectedly risen, contrary to earlier expectations. Electrolyzer costs have also increased dramatically in the past year alone by about 50–60 percent (BNEF, 2024).

In the case of electrolyzers, our estimates reflect the use of higher-cost Western technologies that allow modulation down to nearly 0 percent of capacity. Cheaper alternatives from China are available but are limited in their ability to operate below 40 percent of capacity. To overcome this limitation, it would be necessary to invest in costly electricity storage that would reduce the variability of electricity supply to the electrolyzer. Additionally, for processes like the Haber-Bosch method, the minimum operating load for hydrogen production is around 40 percent. This minimum load constraint contributes to higher LCOH cost, as it increases the required hydrogen storage capacity, which we include in the LCOH calculation.

Moreover, the way we optimize the off-grid renewable energy value chain also contributes to the elevated LCOH. We assume a curtailment of electricity of around 10-15 percent. This deliberate overplanning ensures a more reliable energy supply but also results in an effective increase in the cost of electricity, driving up the cost of hydrogen. While solar energy is relatively cheap on a per-kilowatt-hour basis, its lower full load hours make it less attractive as the sole energy source for hydrogen production. A project relying entirely on solar power could theoretically achieve a hydrogen production cost as low as €1 per kilogram, but this would not be viable due to the limited operational hours.

Overall, the LCOH estimates in our study are a reflection of a more comprehensive and current approach that includes full value chain costs, up-to-date renewable energy market dynamics, and the technical constraints of electrolyzers and ammonia/methanol production, and energy generation infrastructure.
