Scenario combines each port’s strengths and has been used further in the financial and economic analysis. It has the following characteristics:

•   Production of both hydrogen and ammonia takes place in Tan-Tan.

•   A pipeline will connect Tan-Tan with Mohammedia for hydrogen storage in the salt caverns.

•   This pipeline will connect with Jorf Lasfar to serve the local industry.

•   Ammonia will be shipped from Tan-Tan towards Jorf Lasfar for potential bunkering and local use.

•   Ammonia will also be shipped from Tan-Tan to Tanger Med for bunkering.

•   Tan-Tan will export ammonia.

•   Methanol will be produced with the competitively produced hydrogen from Tan-Tan at the
    location where CO2 becomes available. Jorf Lasfar has an industry that could potentially provide
    captured CO2. A hydrogen pipeline connection to Jorf Lasfar would then be recommended.
    Further research is needed to learn more about potential CO2 sources.

•   If a hydrogen pipeline connecting Tan-Tan with Tanger Med is in place, methanol can be shipped
    to or produced in Tanger Med. This pipeline could be extended in the future to function as
    Morocco’s hydrogen backbone and potentially serve the export market in Europe.

This is shown in Figure 6.13 (Scenario 3). This concept is the most cost-effective. It optimally integrates the value chain and perfectly uses the individual ports’ unique selling points, demand drivers, and implementation options. This scenario is, therefore, treated as the Lighthouse Scenario.
It is studied in further detail in the following chapters of this report.
