7.3 Closing the gap
The question is: to what extent, how, and when will the viability gap close? Several factors can contribute to this in their own way:

•      Decline of ammonia production cost: first movers bear the brunt of the costs of implementing
       new technologies, while followers benefit from the industry progressing through learning curves.

•      Concessional financing of projects: providing either grants or soft loans to investment projects
       for parts of the value chain will reduce the costs.

•      Fossil fuels become more expensive, either because of regular market developments or through
       policy measures.

•      Contracts for difference schemes, where the government provides subsidies to cover the
       difference between the buying and selling price of green hydrogen-based fuels.

•      Premiums for green transportation and products paid by customers of maritime transporters
       and industries.

In this section, we elaborate on each of these factors.

7.3.1 Declining production costs
The International Renewable Energy Agency expects costs of green ammonia to decline by approximately 20 percent from 2030 to 2040 and by approximately one-third by 2050 compared to 2030 (IRENA and AEA, 2022). This would bring prices to roughly 2-3x the current costs of
VLSFO or gray ammonia. Note that existing installations would not benefit much from reduced costs unless they are expanded gradually (or partially replaced). The market analysis described in Chapter 4 shows how projected volumes of green hydrogen-based fuels in both the bunkering and export markets will grow until 2050. Both markets have a large range of volumes, making it difficult to predict if, when, and how much green hydrogen-based fuels production capacity will be expanded. Assuming average volumes in the base case for the period 2040–50 are leading,15 and assuming learning curves will reduce the costs of installing solar photovoltaics, onshore wind, and electrolyzers, the LCOA for such an expansion would be approximately €1,000 per ton or €2,200 per ton of VLSFO equivalent (keeping all else constant).16

