7.3.2 Grants and concessional finance
The levelized costs in the scenario analysis are all based on a (nominal) weighted average cost of capital (WACC) of 11.5 percent. The World Bank has indicated that this discount rate is appropriate for the current analysis. It was suggested in a study by the Climate Policy Initiative (2023). The rate of 11.5 percent represents the outcome of an approach to reduce the country risks. According to this study, an even more favorable approach results in a WACC of 8.5 percent. The LCOA of €1,089 per ton at a WACC of 11.5 percent decreases to €977 per ton when a WACC of 8.5 percent is applied. Without any de-risking measures, the study estimates a WACC of 13.5 percent for climate investments in Morocco. Providing one-off construction grants is another option, though these would need to be very substantial (several billion Euros) to significantly impact the LCOA.

Concessional financing of projects, such as providing either grants or soft loans to investment projects for parts of the value chain, can reduce costs. As the prefeasibility study discusses, concessional finance can be important in developing value chains for green hydrogen-based fuels.
This is especially true for the most capital-intensive components in the chain, such as the production of renewable electricity, electrolysis, and the production of hydrogen carriers like e-methanol and e-ammonia.

Concessional finance is typically provided by multilateral development banks. A range of instruments (grants, guarantees, loans, and even equity) can fall under the umbrella of concessional finance. They have in common the fact that the conditions deviate from typical market conditions offered by private and commercial investors and lenders. The goal is generally to advance a project that delivers specific national, regional, or global results, such as decarbonization. Once it has been demonstrated that a viability gap exists, meaning that the project is not financially viable without public support, it can be assessed which form and level of concessional finance is needed.

For example, different levels of concessional finance can significantly impact the LCOH. Combining concessional public debt, concessional private debt, and project grants can reduce the LCOH to competitive levels with conventional energy sources. This can aid in de-risking and mobilizing additional financing for climate action. Concessional finance helps leverage private capital to develop and scale new climate-smart technologies and business models in emerging markets.

The World Bank provides guidelines for the use of concessional finance through the Climate
Investment Funds. These funds are used to support projects that contribute to climate goals, such as decarbonization and the development of renewable energy infrastructure. Additionally, the
World Bank supports projects through Emission Reduction Crediting agreements, which are results-
based payment instruments established under Article 6 of the Paris Agreement. These agreements can provide advance payments on an exceptional basis, helping to finance projects that generate verified emission reductions or mitigation outcomes.

By leveraging these concessional finance instruments, projects can reduce their WACC and
LCOH, making them more financially viable and attractive to private investors. This approach is particularly relevant for capital-intensive components of green hydrogen value chains, such as renewable electricity production and electrolysis.
