7.3.3 Higher prices of current fuels
The 2023 World Energy Outlook by the International Energy Agency (IEA) assumes declining crude oil prices from 2022 to 2030 and 2050 in all three scenarios, expressed in 2022 real prices, and expects a market deficit to put upward pressure on prices (IEA, 2023a).

A study by the International Monetary Fund shows how different demand- and supply-side impacts of energy transition policies can significantly affect oil prices both upward and downward
(IMF, 2023). When considering both (opposing) forces, the resulting oil prices in 2030 are estimated at $70 or $85 per barrel, depending on which policies are assumed to be implemented.

We conclude that there is no compelling reason to assume an increase in the price of VLSFO due to market forces alone. A similar argument applies to the prices of gray ammonia.

Other mechanisms driving up the costs of VLSFO and gray ammonia for the end user are policies and regulations, which impose a price on GHG emissions.

Using a set of assumptions, a Haskoning analysis that assesses the impact of EU ETS shows that the carbon price required to break even when sailing on ammonia compared to fuel oil declines from approximately €545 to 410 to 325 per ton CO2 in 2030, 2040, and 2050, respectively.17 Contrasting these values with the CO2 prices (Figure 7.2) that IEA uses in the Global Energy and Climate Model shows that the required carbon price exceeds the prices in all three scenarios used by IEA,18 although carbon pricing, of course, does help close the gap.

Under the IMO’s approved global economic measure (still to be adopted), ships emitting above specific GHG fuel intensity threshold will need to acquire remedial units to balance their emission deficits. Initially, these will be priced at $100 per t CO2eq and $380 per t CO2eq, depending on how high the emission deficit is (IMO, 2025a).

