Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                                  106

Germany recently opened a €4 billion bidding round for Carbon Contracts for Difference, incentivizing industrial users to switch to green hydrogen (or other low-emission technologies).
Germany is expected to make €50 billion available for the program. Germany’s H2 Global program provides €900 million for the first round of auctions of green derivatives, with further funding earmarked by the German and Dutch governments.

Meanwhile, the European Hydrogen Bank is funded with €3 billion, which is, among other things, meant to organize supply-side auctions with a maximum support level of €4.5 per kg of hydrogen.20
Outside the EU, major importing markets such as Korea and Japan also develop contracts for difference schemes.

It is too early to tell exactly how much funding under which conditions will be available post-2030 when exports could start coming online. The question is also about which entities in which sectors and jurisdictions would be eligible for such subsidies. However, contracts for difference could have a substantial positive impact on bridging the viability gap for end-users of green hydrogen-based fuels.

7.3.5 Green transportation premiums
While the incremental shipping costs due to green hydrogen-based fuels adoption are significant in absolute terms, transportation costs often constitute a relatively small part of the goods’ value. This was quantified in a study by Transport & Environment (2022), which focused on Fuel EU Maritime’s target for GHG intensity declining from -6 percent to -14 percent. Their results showed increased seaborne transportation costs of €8.7–13.4 per TEU for a simulated trip from China to Belgium.
For goods not directly sold to consumers, such as in the shipping of bulk products, it may be more difficult to pass on the higher costs of green hydrogen-based fuels.

7.4 Sensitivity analysis
The scenario analysis results in LCOH/LCOA estimates underpinned by various assumptions.
The assumptions are adjusted to test the LCOH/LCOA estimates, and the degree of change in the results—the sensitivity—is found. The results are most sensitive to a change in the CAPEX of installing onshore wind capacity and electrolyzers.

To calculate LCOH, all costs associated with hydrogen production are considered, including CAPEX and operational expenditures (OPEX). These costs are then divided by the total amount of hydrogen produced over the plant’s lifespan, with adjustments made for the time value of money using a discount rate. This means that both costs and production amounts are converted into their present value to accurately reflect their worth over time.

Similarly, the calculation method for LCOA mirrors that of LCOH. It involves summing all the costs associated with ammonia production, including CAPEX and OPEX, and dividing these by the total ammonia output over the plant’s lifespan. Like LCOH, this calculation also uses a discount rate to adjust both costs and production amounts to their present value, ensuring an accurate measure of the economic feasibility of ammonia production projects.

