8.2.1 Regulatory frameworks

To support the transition to green hydrogen-based bunker fuels, the government should ensure that national regulations address environmental and social risks and create an attractive investment climate. International regulation, whether based on energy consumption, fuel carbon content, or emissions pricing, may have a significant impact on the uptake of green marine fuels in the next decades.

To investigate:
•   Measures to support the financial viability of green hydrogen (derivatives): Currently,
    green hydrogen-based (bunker) fuels are not financially viable when compared to fossil fuels.
    To address the viability gap, the study recommends exploring different options to reduce the
    costs of electricity supply, one of the largest components of upfront capital investment.
    This, combined with concessional finance, carbon pricing, carbon contracts for difference,
    and green transportation premiums, can help in closing the viability gap.

To act upon:
•   National guidelines on green hydrogen-based (bunker) fuel handling operations: Developing
    these guidelines would enhance safety during operations. It would also clarify procedures to port
    authorities, terminal operators, and project developers, facilitate knowledge sharing, and help
    ensure a level playing field among ports. Such guidelines could build on and consider relevant
    guidelines developed by the IMO (see Chapter 3.1.1 for some examples) and by classification
    societies, and incorporate lessons learned and best practices from ports that already have
    experience handling fuels (like Mohammedia) and ammonia (like Jorf Lasfar). Please see the
    elaboration of the approach applied in the Netherlands (elaborated in Appendix A7) as an
    example that could inform guidelines in Morocco.

•   National Hydrogen Roadmap with regular updates: The first Hydrogen Roadmap was published
    in 2021. Since then, the national landscape and assumptions behind the modelling have changed
    significantly because of developments such as Offre Maroc and OCP’s plans for green ammonia
    production. It is therefore recommended that the National Hydrogen Roadmap is updated every
    five years to consider new developments.

•   Attractive investment climate for methanol production: If Morocco wishes to develop methanol
    production projects, an attractive investment climate must be created. This can be done,
    for example, by establishing an effective domestic carbon pricing scheme to comply with EU
    regulations on permitted carbon sources (see Box 2.1), allocating land at competitive locations,
    providing a clear permitting process, and, if needed, facilitating a CO2 import terminal.

•   Environmental and social impact assessment: Hydrogen-related developments pose safety and
    environmental risks that may create social resistance, especially given the hazardous nature of
    hydrogen materials and the spatial needs for safe facility siting. In 2020, Morocco adopted law
    49-17 on environmental assessment mandating a public inquiry of at least 10 days. This inquiry
    allows citizens to make observations and proposals on the possible impact of the project on
    the environment (Ministère de la Transition Energétique et du Développement Durable, 2022).
    For comprehensive project planning, cumulative impacts, water management, occupational
    and community health, ecosystem services, and space constraints for brownfield versus
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade             115

    greenfield sites must be considered. A more complete list is available in Appendix A.8 and in the
    scoping study conducted in Latin America by Signoria and Barlettani (2023). Early stakeholder
    consultations and participative project design are recommended to align projects with both
    environmental and social standards.

To monitor:
•   International and regional regulation: The regulatory framework will be the driving force for
    establishing the hydrogen and derivatives market. Therefore, international regulatory dynamics
    should be monitored to ensure that Morocco can capitalize on emerging opportunities and that
    the offerings made are in line with market demand. Discussions at the IMO and EU levels will
    be most relevant for the development and shaping of the marine hydrogen-based fuel market.
    At the IMO, it is recommended to follow, in particular, the development of policies to reduce GHG
    emissions from ships (including on how to spend potential revenues from an emissions pricing
    instrument) and guidelines on the lifecycle GHG intensity of marine fuels (see Chapter 3.1.1).
    Regarding EU policies, staying informed of regulatory developments for the Fit for 55 Package,
    REPowerEU, and EU Hydrogen Strategy will be important. This enables adequate decision-
    making on infrastructure development. At the IMO, Morocco could also actively participate in
    driving and shaping the relevant regulatory debates.

•   National regulation: To understand Morocco’s potential for industry demand, monitoring critical
    developments in the regulatory framework for the industry in Morocco and key demand factors,
    such as the requirements from off-takers of Moroccan products produced with green energy,
    is recommended. It will also be critical to monitor the developments around Offre Maroc; Masen
    is the executing agency, and close cooperation is recommended. Furthermore, the Ministry
    of Energy Transition and Sustainable Development and the National Office of Hydrocarbons
    and Mines are anticipated to take a lead role in developing the energy networks. GIZ has
    commissioned a legal study on the gaps in Morocco’s regulation concerning green fuels, which
    could provide useful input for identifying areas of work.

8.2.2 Fuel production and handling
The successful production and handling of green marine fuels involves addressing the sourcing of key feedstocks, such as CO2 and water, and ensuring that the workforce has the necessary skills for handling these fuels. By investigating value chains, scaling production, and optimizing operational processes using lessons learned from smaller projects, safe and efficient production of these fuels can be ensured.

To investigate:
•   Sourcing of CO2 for methanol production: It is likely that in the very near future, shipping will
    demand methanol as a bunker fuel. To cater to that demand, viable and accredited carbon
    sources will be needed for methanol production. GIZ has launched a study evaluating available
    carbon sources and carbon demand for hydrogen-based fuel production in five Moroccan regions
    (Tanmia, 2024). The study results will likely be available in 2025. The study’s progress should
    be followed closely. In addition, potential carbon sources in other countries and the viability of
    importing CO2 from those countries should be explored.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                116

•   Availability of desalinated water: The production of hydrogen requires large volumes
    of freshwater as feedstock. The Offre Maroc (Royaume du Maroc, 2024) has tasked the
    government authorities in charge of water, the budget, and the National Office of Electricity and
    Water, with mapping the patterns of complementarity between the desalinated water needs
    of green hydrogen projects within the framework of the Offre Maroc and existing and future
    desalination plants.

To act upon:
•   Training and upskilling of a workforce capable of handling hydrogen and derivatives:
    To successfully produce, store, bunker, and export hydrogen-based fuels in ports, a workforce
    capable of handling such fuels will be needed. This may require both additional capacity and new
    skills. Informed by input from the individual ports and industry, training programs and dedicated
    facilities are recommended to be developed to equip port personnel with the necessary expertise.
    It may be possible to draw from the experience of handling ammonia in Jorf Lasfar and transfer
    this knowledge to the other ports. According to the Offre Maroc (Royaume du Maroc, 2024),
    the government authority in charge of Industry and Trade is responsible for assessing,
    among other things, human resource needs related to the green hydrogen sector.

•   National CO2 sourcing plan: Depending on the outcome of investigating (national and regional)
    carbon sources and the monitoring of regulatory treatment of such sources, a national CO2
    sourcing plan could be developed.

To monitor:
•   Experience with future fuels: There is high uncertainty around future fuel choices. While most
    literature suggests that methanol and particularly ammonia will be the options that, in the long
    run, will ensure scalable and lowest cost compliance with a net zero trajectory for deep sea
    shipping, these fuels are currently still emerging, assessed in studies and trialed in small projects.
    Monitoring and following the knowledge developed and experience gained about these fuels,
    in particular in real-life operating conditions in ports leading on this subject (such as Singapore
    and Rotterdam), will be important. Relatedly, the commercial scale availability of technologies
    needed for ships to operate on green hydrogen-based bunker fuels should be monitored.

•   Key pilot projects: Several pilot and reference projects are being executed in Morocco and
    abroad. In Morocco, it is recommended to monitor and share the results and lessons learned from
    pilot and reference projects being developed by Masen as well as pilot projects on electrolyzers
    and Power-to-Liquid production being conducted by IRESEN. The IRESEN pilot project, located
    in the Energy Platform of IRESEN in the Ben Guerir area, is a pioneering project that will serve
    as a critical step in advancing renewable energy production and hydrogen-based technologies.
    This project features a 20 kW alkaline electrolyzer powered by a photovoltaic plant and includes
    small-scale reactors for ammonia and methanol production. The IRESEN pilot will provide
    valuable insights into integrating renewable energy and hydrogen systems. As a first-of-its-kind
    platform, this project must be closely monitored to gather key lessons for scaling similar future
    initiatives. Internationally, ammonia handling and refueling pilots, particularly in Japan and
    Singapore, are worth following.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                 117

8.2.3 Infrastructure
The government plays a role in developing common-use infrastructure that would maximize the co-benefits of hydrogen investments. To develop a green hydrogen economy, it is advantageous to provide shared infrastructure that enables the utilization of hydrogen at the lowest cost.
This includes, for example, port infrastructure built to import large renewable energy equipment, such as wind blades, and export green hydrogen-based fuels, or a hydrogen pipeline backbone for transporting hydrogen cheaply between key off-takers.

To investigate:
•   Development of hydrogen pipeline infrastructure:

    –   The Lighthouse Scenario recommends a pipeline from the Tan-Tan province to the salt
        caverns in Mohammedia and potentially an extension to Jorf Lasfar and Tanger Med for
        methanol production. This pipeline, combined with storage in salt caverns, could enable
        Morocco to offer an efficient hydrogen storage mechanism for hydrogen produced in
        Tan-Tan. Furthermore, it could form the first stage of a north-south national hydrogen
        backbone and potentially connect to European networks. If the pipeline is not developed in
        time, project developers may opt for buried pipe storage instead of utilizing the salt caverns.
        Once buried pipe storage is available, there will be little incentive to transition to a pipeline.
        It is recommended to further study and discuss this opportunity at the national level in
        cooperation with the responsible authorities.
    –   In the medium to long term, the Scenario recommends developing a backbone of hydrogen
        pipelines to serve the country, specifically to supply pure green hydrogen to the steel and
        fertilizer industries in Jorf Lasfar and other industrial hubs. It is recommended that the
        viability of such a hydrogen pipeline system be further investigated, taking into consideration
        the potential of other ports and industrial hubs to contribute to the hydrogen value chain.
        According to the Offre Maroc (Royaume du Maroc, 2024), the government authorities in
        charge of energy, budget, and the National Office of Hydrocarbons and Mines are responsible
        for carrying out studies relating to a national network of hydrogen pipelines and transformed
        gas pipelines intended to be connected to the European green hydrogen network.

•   Hydrogen storage in salt caverns and geological salt layer deposits: The Lighthouse Scenario
    suggests storing hydrogen in salt caverns in Mohammedia. However, a map in the Offre Maroc
    also shows other potential saline caverns for storage, including those close to Jorf Lasfar.
    According to the Offre Maroc (Royaume du Maroc, 2024), the government authorities in charge
    of equipment, water and energy, budget, as well as the National Office of Hydrocarbons and
    Mines, are responsible for carrying out studies relating to the identified salt caverns.

8.2.4 Other
Beyond the core areas of regulatory frameworks, infrastructure, and fuel production, several additional actions will help strengthen Morocco’s green hydrogen value chain. These include fostering collaboration between Moroccan ports, monitoring hydrogen developments in neighboring regions, and considering broader external factors such as global market dynamics.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade              118

To act upon:
•   Enhanced collaboration between Moroccan ports: Realizing a complete hydrogen-based fuel
    value chain between the four ports can be done most cost-effectively by utilizing the unique
    advantages of each port. This may also be true for other ports. Therefore, it is recommended
    to continue fostering collaboration among the ports to ensure a level playing field, knowledge
    sharing, collaboration, and efficiency within the value chains.

To monitor:
•   Hydrogen developments in nearby regions: This study focuses on four ports and their
    hinterlands. Renewable energy and hydrogen projects are being studied and developed in other
    locations and nearby areas, including in different countries. Such projects could contribute to
    a national hydrogen value chain or compete with the four ports. Therefore, it is recommended
    that hydrogen developments in nearby locations and neighboring regions be monitored to assess
    potential risks and identify possible synergies.

To consider:
•   External factors impacting the hydrogen market: The global market for renewable hydrogen
    has been impacted by recent macroeconomic developments such as inflation and costs of
    capital. Costs of labor, materials, renewable energy, and shortages in the value chains have
    contributed to a substantial increase in costs recently (IEA, 2023b). Yet, the long-term outlook
    remains positive, with significant cost reductions still anticipated until 2050 due to technological
    change and efficiency improvements (Hydrogen Council, 2023). These developments are of
    crucial importance for the viability of renewable hydrogen globally.

•   Surveys and programs to understand and improve job creation: Job creation in the hydrogen
    value chain that involves long-term skilled work may include “indirect” jobs, jobs in the supply
    chains for renewable energy, electrolyzers, and other manufacturing, not necessarily located
    within Morocco. First, industry-level analyses could be conducted to identify segments of the
    green hydrogen-based fuels supply chain where the country or region could be competitive.
    Second, surveying to periodically quantify and characterize jobs created by relevant projects and
    noting the skills required and durations of the jobs may help create pilot programs to improve
    job creation and local and regional economic development. These programs should strive to
    identify the factors necessary for job creation and develop theories of change that help identify
    the causal links that lead to jobs. This should help with the design of technical and vocational
    education training programs in line with the needs of the market. Due to rapid technological
    development, sustained financing from the government may be required to ensure that industry-
    level strategies and training programs are up-to-date.

