8.4 Green hydrogen-based bunker fuel pilot in Tanger Med

8.4.1 Pilot set-up
A pilot project in Tanger Med is proposed to explore the feasibility of ship-to-ship bunkering using alternative fuels. This pilot aims to build experience with operational procedures and regulations around methanol (and ammonia). The pilot will provide insight and practice safety procedures, demonstrate port readiness level for green fuel bunkering, and identify space that should be reserved for this activity in the masterplan. This further includes building up experience with securing the fuel volumes.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                     128

It is recommended to adhere to safety protocols and engage stakeholders throughout the development process. This is part of the roadmap recommendations to ensure transparency with local residents and others. The pilot activities are relatively small, and the safety risks are comparable to those associated with the current handling of fossil fuels.

Besides safety protocols, the technical aspects of bunkering and storage equipment need to be addressed during the pilot’s operational phase.

On environmental and social aspects, the pilot contributes by ensuring early-on engagement with local communities, environmental groups, and stakeholders. By scheduling regular updates and feedback sessions, concerns can be identified and addressed before the full-scale implementation.

The same applies to the regulatory aspects. By working closely with regulatory bodies, compliance with relevant regulations will be addressed early on, and the timeline for obtaining necessary permits and approvals can be optimized. Although a pilot will never be fully viable, addressing these elements can help mitigate risks and improve the overall feasibility of the pilot and the project.

To limit the investment costs and the impact of the pilot, it is recommended to:

•    Use temporary (rented) or existing facilities.

•    If possible, the pilot facilities should be in the same location as the projected final location and
     develop according to full-scale specifications, allowing the investment to have a permanent
     character.

Initially, the pilot will be set up with methanol, as ships running on ammonia are currently unavailable. Once technical developments allow ammonia as bunker fuel, the pilot project can be executed with ammonia. If technical developments do not allow for ammonia-fueled vessels by the time the first stage of the pilot is finalized, it is recommended to run the operations using methanol, though mimicking that the fuel handled is ammonia. Since the pilot is aimed at practicing bunkering operations of alternative fuels, sourcing/importing of CO2 for methanol production is not included; it would divert attention from the overall goal of the pilot.

The findings must be logged accurately during the pilot. This helps identify challenges and potential improvement options. Once the pilot is completed, lessons learned can be shared with other ports.

The pilot practice operations are depicted in Figure 8.3 and consist of the following steps:

1.   Importing methanol by ship

     For the pilot, the methanol will be imported by ship to Tanger Med; the origin of the fuel is
     assumed to be Spain or Morocco via (leased) vessel. It is recommended that a small tanker
     of around 2,000 deadweight tonnage (if possible, even smaller) be sourced since the required
     storage volume of the pilot project is limited. At berth, the methanol is offloaded to a truck
     (ship-to-truck) and transported to the storage. This step of the pilot allows practicing the
     offloading operations of a methanol vessel, including safety arrangements.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                           129

2. Methanol storage in temporary containers

       Methanol is offloaded from the carrier directly into a truck. The truck transports the methanol
       to the storage. In the final setup, methanol or ammonia will be stored in tanks. However, this is
       impractical for a pilot project since a tank is a permanent structure. For this reason, methanol
       will be stored in containers during the pilot. These can be acquired easily and recycled once the
       pilot is finished. Furthermore, they offer flexibility and meet international safety standards for
       transporting hazardous substances. Normally, the offtake volume would be based on the size of
       the methanol vessel. However, in the case of the pilot, the proposed storage capacity is limited, and
       therefore, the volume of one cycle equals the storage volume. Assuming 30 TEU containers with
       a volume of 33 m3, each result in handling approximately 1,000 m3 (=1,000,000 liters) methanol
       per cycle.

3. Filling up bunker barges with methanol

       The pilot bunker barge will be loaded with methanol directly from the storage facilities.
       This requires the construction of offloading facilities and the availability of a bunker barge.
       This pilot step allows practicing loading operations of a bunker vessel, including safety
       arrangements and staff training.

4. Bunkering procedure of ship-to-ship bunkering, both at quay and offshore

       Ship-to-ship bunkering can be done using mobile or static bunker barges moored alongside the
       ship while it is loaded. A vessel running on methanol will be leased and will act as the end-user
       (alias the ship being loaded). Initially, bunkering operations are practiced at the quay, and once
       these are running smoothly, the pilot can be run with offshore bunkering. This will provide
       valuable insights into ship-to-ship bunkering operations.

This four-step cycle will be repeated 10 times. The first cycle will take longer, as all operations are new, but the operational time will decrease over time. After completing 10 cycles, the operations should run smoothly and simulate a realistic final situation.

