8.5 Conclusion
The previous chapters have identified a Lighthouse Scenario which represents the most cost-effective combination of functions performed by each of the four ports studied. This chapter outlined the next steps to realize this Lighthouse Scenario.

Towards this end, a distinct role was suggested for each port, namely for Tan-Tan to become a hydrogen and alternative fuel production and export hub, for Jorf Lasfar to become an important off-taker of hydrogen and ammonia, for Mohammedia to store hydrogen in its salt caverns and for
Tanger Med to become an alternative fuel production and bunkering hub.

To enable the ports to fulfill this role in the future, short-, medium, and long-term ambitions were identified. In the short term, that is before 2030, it is suggested to focus on launching the initial phases to scale hydrogen, to explore carbon sources for methanol production, and to execute two pilots, one for ship-to-ship bunkering in Tanger Med and another one to store hydrogen in
Mohammedia’s salt caverns. In the following decade, ammonia and/or methanol should be produced at a large scale in Tan-Tan, and various options should be developed to transport these products to the other ports. Beyond 2040, ammonia and/or methanol could also be produced in Mohammedia,
Tanger Med and Jorf Lasfar (depending on carbon being available) and a national hydrogen backbone should be established which could also be connected to other countries.

Focusing on the short-term ambitions, the remainder of the chapter identifies general and port-specific recommendations to act upon, investigate, monitor, and consider. Here, several recommendations recur, such as:

•   Fostering greater collaboration and knowledge sharing among ports, ministries,
    and other stakeholders.

•   Developing guidelines on the safe handling of green hydrogen-based fuels.

•   Exploring carbon sources for methanol production.

•   Providing information on planned port developments in Tan-Tan.

•   Participating in a green corridor initiative.

•   Training the workforce in the safe handling of alternative fuels.

•   Monitoring regulatory and hydrogen developments at the international level.

Finally, two possible pilots are identified: one close to the port of Mohammedia and another one in Tanger Med. The Mohammedia pilot focuses on using salt caverns for hydrogen storage, but because it is not the port authority who will run this pilot, only a concise pilot summary is provided.
The Tanger Med pilot explores the feasibility of ship-to-ship bunkering using alternative fuels. It aims to gain experience with operational procedures and regulations regarding methanol (and ammonia).
A roadmap for the Tanger Med pilot project has been elaborated, outlining the pilot’s setup and the activities recommended at different times. CAPEX and OPEX have also been estimated. The physical execution of the pilot is anticipated to take one year in total.
Conclusion

Morocco has the potential to reinvent itself as a green hydrogen bunker fuel hub if it takes actions in the short, medium, and long term such as developing pilot projects, working on its infrastructure, and ultimately scaling up production.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade              137

It is imperative for the world economy to decarbonize by mid-century, if we are to limit global warming to well below 2°C and below 1.5°C. For some industries, such as global shipping, steel, and fertilizers, this will partly be achieved by switching to green hydrogen. This report focuses on green ammonia and green methanol as hydrogen derivatives, with a specific emphasis on their use as maritime bunker fuel, export commodities, or as fuel/feedstock in industrial complexes near the ports. It provides an overview of potential future roles of the selected ports within the green hydrogen value chain, focusing on Tanger Med, Jorf Lasfar, Mohammedia, and a potential greenfield port near Tan-Tan.

The report begins with a supply and demand side analysis of green ammonia and green methanol.
It maps the opportunities for production, storage, bunker supply, export, and supply to nearby industries. Over 20 scenarios were developed, and a Lighthouse Scenario was identified as the most cost-effective option, integrating the value chain and making optimal use of the unique selling points, demand drivers, and implementation options of the individual ports. Finally, a series of checkpoints was identified for 2030, 2040, and 2050.

9.1 Market demand and supply
The demand for renewable hydrogen and its derivatives is expected to grow, and the North Africa region has been identified as having production center potential for serving demand centers in
Europe. Morocco is well positioned to be a major player in the production of green hydrogen due to its location relative to Europe, its potential for low-cost renewable energy, and its potential for hydrogen export infrastructure through existing ports and abundant coastline. In particular, its excellent solar and wind conditions make it well-placed to generate renewable energy and hydrogen and its derivatives at costs lower or similar to other North African countries.

9.2 The role of each port
Each port has distinct characteristics and advantages. For example, the bunker market potential is mainly found in Tanger Med, as well as in European bunkering hubs such as Rotterdam, Antwerp,
Gibraltar, and Algeciras. Tanger Med is currently the only Moroccan port where large-scale bunkering takes place; this is expected to remain the case due to its strategic location at the crossroads of important maritime routes. Mohammedia, specializing in crude and refined products, is in proximity to proven salt caverns that may offer attractive storage for pressurized hydrogen in the future. Jorf Lasfar already handles around 2 mt of ammonia every year and, with its proximity to several industrial complexes, would be well positioned to become a green hydrogen or green fuels hub. Tan-Tan offers no direct possibilities for off-take, but sufficient space for new developments, and superior wind and solar conditions to cost-effectively produce green hydrogen.

The report provides an overview of more than 20 potential value chain scenarios, which focus on the production, storage, bunker supply, export, and supply to nearby industries related to the selected hydrogen derivatives. The study has identified a Lighthouse Scenario, which offers the most cost-effective opportunity for the development of a green hydrogen value chain.
Gateway to Green Energy: Moroccan Ports as Hubs for Hydrogen Fuel Development and Trade                 138

The Lighthouse Scenario, which comprises the production of hydrogen and ammonia in Tan-Tan, with connections to Mohammedia and Jorf Lasfar for storage, export, and industry offtake, was recommended as the most cost-effective option.

9.3 Financial viability
The financial viability of green hydrogen-based bunker fuels in Morocco is a critical aspect that requires careful consideration. The study has highlighted that while ammonia and methanol produced and supplied in or exported from Morocco will be competitive compared to other sources, affordability remains a concern. The costs of unabated fossil fuels in both the bunker fuels market and the ammonia market for fertilizer present a substantial viability gap. A combination of developments and measures will be required to make green hydrogen, green methanol, and green ammonia financially viable.

To address the viability gap, the study recommends exploring different options targeted at reducing the costs of electricity supply, as these have a strong impact on the project’s economics.
This, in combination with concessional finance, carbon pricing, carbon contracts for difference, and green transportation premiums, can contribute to closing the viability gap. Additionally, the need for shipping lines to comply with regulations from the EU, the IMO, and other jurisdictions is expected to drive the uptake of green hydrogen-based bunker fuels.

9.4 Regulation and policy implications
The regulatory framework is identified as a driving force to establish a hydrogen and derivatives market. It split the regulations required into two groups: regulation to incentivize green fuel production, and practical safety and handling guidelines to ensure the proper handling of fuels in ports.

The Offre Maroc, a regulatory framework recently issued, serves as an incentive, and facilitates a streamlined process for the development of renewable energy projects. It outlines the scope, available land, and governance of the sector, aiming to attract national and international investors interested in the production of green hydrogen. Monitoring international regulatory dynamics and markets to ensure that the offerings made are aligned with market demand, in particular at the IMO, is recommended. Additionally, monitoring carbon prices will be crucial in increasing the market for green hydrogen-based bunker fuels and creating more scale.

The study further emphasizes the need for national guidelines on safety related to the handling and storage of green hydrogen-based bunker fuels to ensure a level playing field and knowledge sharing.
